Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell["Quantum Contextuality", "Section",ExpressionUUID->"43ab2b2f-2bc0-7e4c-9155-c0261088f35c"],

Cell["\<\
Quantum mechanics predicts correlations between measurement outcomes that no \
theory built only on pre-agreed, shared randomness can reproduce \[LongDash] \
a genuine, provable advantage with no classical analogue. The cleanest way to \
see this is as a game: a referee poses a question to each of several players \
who cannot communicate once play begins; classically, the best they can do is \
agree beforehand on a joint strategy, possibly using shared randomness (a \
\[OpenCurlyDoubleQuote]hidden variable\[CloseCurlyDoubleQuote]); quantum \
mechanically, sharing an entangled state lets them win more often than ANY \
such classical strategy allows. This gap \[LongDash] a hard ceiling for every \
classical strategy, broken by a quantum one \[LongDash] is the resource the \
rest of this essay is built on.\
\>", "Text",ExpressionUUID->"7877c2d0-5bdf-a940-9e51-c3b6a21cf958"],

Cell[TextData[{
 StyleBox["\[FilledRightTriangle] ",
  FontSize->10,
  FontColor->GrayLevel[0.55]],
 StyleBox["entanglement",
  FontSize->11,
  FontWeight->"Bold",
  FontColor->GrayLevel[0.3]],
 StyleBox["  \[Bullet] entangled state, entangled pair, non-separable state, \
QuantumEntangledQ check",
  FontSize->10,
  FontColor->GrayLevel[0.45]]
}], "Text",
 ShowGroupOpener->True,
 CellMargins->{{86, 30}, {1, 
  5}},ExpressionUUID->"0396f3cf-2b47-e045-bd76-9186348adf20"],

Cell["\<\
A joint state of two systems A and B is a product state when it factorizes as \
|\[Psi]\:27e9 = |\[CurlyPhi]A\:27e9 \[CircleTimes] |\[CurlyPhi]B\:27e9 \
\[LongDash] system A in some state and system B in some state, separately. A \
pure state is entangled exactly when no such factorization exists; a mixed \
state is entangled when it is not even a probabilistic mixture of product \
states (not separable). The Bell pair (|00\:27e9+|11\:27e9)/\[Sqrt]2, \
prepared here by H then CNOT, is entangled in this sense \[LongDash] the \
property QuantumEntangledQ certifies \[LongDash] and is the resource lifting \
the CHSH game from the classical ceiling 3/4 to cos\.b2(\[Pi]/8) \
\[TildeTilde] 0.854, without permitting signaling: neither party's local \
statistics change.\
\>", "Text",
 CellMargins->{{104, 30}, {4, 4}},
 FontSize->12,ExpressionUUID->"fad0df67-f3be-704b-b9af-b122a12ff296"],

Cell[TextData[{
 StyleBox["\[FilledRightTriangle] ",
  FontSize->10,
  FontColor->GrayLevel[0.55]],
 StyleBox["local hidden-variable theory",
  FontSize->11,
  FontWeight->"Bold",
  FontColor->GrayLevel[0.3]],
 StyleBox["  \[Bullet] hidden variable, shared randomness, pre-agreed joint \
strategy, local realistic theory",
  FontSize->10,
  FontColor->GrayLevel[0.45]]
}], "Text",
 ShowGroupOpener->True,
 CellMargins->{{86, 30}, {1, 
  5}},ExpressionUUID->"82d4540b-9601-2f4c-bc11-764d394167f7"],

Cell["\<\
A model in which measurement statistics arise from a variable \[Lambda] \
shared in advance from a common source (the essay's \"pre-agreed, shared \
randomness\"), with no signal exchanged between parties once measurements \
begin. Formally, the joint outcome probabilities must factorize: p(a, b | x, \
y, \[Lambda]) = p(a | x, \[Lambda]) \[CenterDot] p(b | y, \[Lambda]), where \
x, y are the local settings and a, b the outcomes; observed statistics are \
averages of these products over a distribution of \[Lambda] independent of \
the setting choices. Determinism is not required \[LongDash] factorizability \
suffices. Bell showed every such model obeys inequalities, such as the CHSH \
bound |S| \[LessEqual] 2, that entangled quantum states violate; the \
single-system, noncontextual analogue is the KCBS bound \[CapitalSigma]p \
\[LessEqual] \[Alpha](C5) = 2.\
\>", "Text",
 CellMargins->{{104, 30}, {4, 4}},
 FontSize->12,ExpressionUUID->"61beeefc-e4ca-9340-b579-22319547212a"],

Cell["\<\
The question in its original form, the one this whole essay answers: given \
only a device's input-output statistics, can its interior ever be proven \
genuinely quantum rather than a classical fake built to match?\
\>", "CodeText",ExpressionUUID->"7ec1d193-105a-0945-961e-bafd64e58f7c"],

