Cell[TextData[{
 StyleBox["\[FilledRightTriangle] ",
  FontSize->10,
  FontColor->GrayLevel[0.55]],
 StyleBox["CHSH inequality",
  FontSize->11,
  FontWeight->"Bold",
  FontColor->GrayLevel[0.3]],
 StyleBox["  \[Bullet] S = E(a0,b0)+E(a0,b1)+E(a1,b0)\[Minus]E(a1,b1), |S| \
\[LessEqual] 2, Clauser\[Dash]Horne\[Dash]Shimony\[Dash]Holt (1969)",
  FontSize->10,
  FontColor->GrayLevel[0.45]]
}], "Text",
 ShowGroupOpener->True,
 CellMargins->{{86, 30}, {1, 
  5}},ExpressionUUID->"fee4277a-38d9-7c40-a4a6-384f8fce3f0e"],

Cell["\<\
A Bell inequality due to Clauser, Horne, Shimony and Holt (1969) for a \
two-party experiment in which Alice and Bob each choose between two \
measurement settings (a0, a1 and b0, b1) and record \[PlusMinus]1 outcomes. \
Writing E(a,b) for the expectation of the product of outcomes, form S = \
E(a0,b0) + E(a0,b1) + E(a1,b0) \[Minus] E(a1,b1). In any local \
hidden-variable theory the outcomes at each setting are fixed values \
\[PlusMinus]1, and for such numbers the combination can never exceed 2 in \
magnitude, so |S| \[LessEqual] 2. Quantum mechanics violates this: an \
entangled pair with optimally rotated settings gives S = 2\[Sqrt]2 \
\[TildeTilde] 2.828, Tsirelson's bound.\
\>", "Text",
 CellMargins->{{104, 30}, {4, 4}},
 FontSize->12,ExpressionUUID->"3ce6cca3-c125-724d-9814-f4c0dcb0b7aa"],

Cell[TextData[{
 StyleBox["\[FilledRightTriangle] ",
  FontSize->10,
  FontColor->GrayLevel[0.55]],
 StyleBox["correlator / expectation value E(a,b)",
  FontSize->11,
  FontWeight->"Bold",
  FontColor->GrayLevel[0.3]],
 StyleBox["  \[Bullet] E(a0,b0)\[Ellipsis]E(a1,b1), \:3008A_k A_(k+1)\:3009, \
correlation sum \[CapitalSigma], four correlators",
  FontSize->10,
  FontColor->GrayLevel[0.45]]
}], "Text",
 ShowGroupOpener->True,
 CellMargins->{{86, 30}, {1, 
  5}},ExpressionUUID->"f16e0ab2-852b-f744-acdb-b269cb20ffeb"],

Cell["\<\
The correlator E(a,b) is the expectation value of the product of the two \
\[PlusMinus]1 measurement outcomes when the stations choose settings a and b: \
statistically, the average of that product over many repeated runs, so E(a,b) \
lies between \[Minus]1 (perfect anticorrelation) and +1 (perfect \
correlation). Quantum mechanics computes it as \:27e8\[Psi]|A\[CircleTimes]B|\
\[Psi]\:27e9 for the observables at those settings \[LongDash] the essay's \
bell.KroneckerProduct[a,b].bell. Bell-type quantities are sums of \
correlators: CHSH uses S = E(a0,b0) + E(a0,b1) + E(a1,b0) \[Minus] E(a1,b1), \
with |S| \[LessEqual] 2 for local hidden variables, while the Bell state \
gives three correlators +1/\[Sqrt]2 and one \[Minus]1/\[Sqrt]2, so S = 2\
\[Sqrt]2; KCBS analogously sums \:27e8A_k A_(k+1)\:27e9 around the pentagon.\
\>", "Text",
 CellMargins->{{104, 30}, {4, 4}},
 FontSize->12,ExpressionUUID->"78fe30d9-e7fe-a441-885f-9f1e573cb7c3"],

Cell["\<\
Bell (1964) showed this is a genuinely testable claim, not just a \
philosophical preference: any theory where outcomes are fixed by shared \
hidden variables, with no signal exchanged between the parties, must obey a \
specific inequality. The version tested in practice is due to Clauser, Horne, \
Shimony and Holt (1969): two separated parties, Alice and Bob, each choose \
one of two measurement settings and record a \[PlusMinus]1 outcome; forming \
the combination S = E(a0,b0) + E(a0,b1) + E(a1,b0) \[Minus] E(a1,b1) of the \
four correlators, every local hidden-variable theory obeys |S| \[LessEqual] \
2. Quantum mechanics, given an entangled pair and the right measurement \
settings, reaches S = 2\[Sqrt]2 \[TildeTilde] 2.828 \[LongDash] Tsirelson's \
bound (Tsirelson, 1980), strictly above 2.\
\>", "Text",ExpressionUUID->"9c7a59a2-7787-a64b-9d08-2758be8636dc"],

Cell[TextData[{
 StyleBox["\[FilledRightTriangle] ",
  FontSize->10,
  FontColor->GrayLevel[0.55]],
 StyleBox["Bell's theorem",
  FontSize->11,
  FontWeight->"Bold",
  FontColor->GrayLevel[0.3]],
 StyleBox["  \[Bullet] Bell (1964), Bell inequality, local hidden-variable \
inequality",
  FontSize->10,
  FontColor->GrayLevel[0.45]]
}], "Text",
 ShowGroupOpener->True,
 CellMargins->{{86, 30}, {1, 
  5}},ExpressionUUID->"3d8690f0-70a4-7c47-aa32-010ab88aa5cd"],

Cell["\<\
Bell's theorem (Bell, 1964) states that no local hidden-variable theory can \
reproduce all predictions of quantum mechanics: if measurement outcomes are \
determined by shared variables fixed at the source, and each party's outcome \
is independent of the other's distant setting choice, the resulting \
correlations must satisfy an inequality that suitable entangled quantum \
states violate. Its most-tested special case is the CHSH inequality (Clauser, \
Horne, Shimony and Holt, 1969): with two settings and \[PlusMinus]1 outcomes \
per party, S = E(a0,b0) + E(a0,b1) + E(a1,b0) \[Minus] E(a1,b1) obeys |S| \
\[LessEqual] 2 in every local hidden-variable theory, while quantum mechanics \
reaches S = 2\[Sqrt]2 (Tsirelson's bound). The theorem is the general no-go \
result; CHSH is one derived inequality.\
\>", "Text",
 CellMargins->{{104, 30}, {4, 4}},
 FontSize->12,ExpressionUUID->"a075a332-32d0-dc4b-83d4-3931b9309b01"],

Cell[TextData[{
 StyleBox["\[FilledRightTriangle] ",
  FontSize->10,
  FontColor->GrayLevel[0.55]],
 StyleBox["Tsirelson bound",
  FontSize->11,
  FontWeight->"Bold",
  FontColor->GrayLevel[0.3]],
 StyleBox["  \[Bullet] 2\[Sqrt]2 \[TildeTilde] 2.828, Tsirelson (1980), \
Cirel'son bound",
  FontSize->10,
  FontColor->GrayLevel[0.45]]
}], "Text",
 ShowGroupOpener->True,
 CellMargins->{{86, 30}, {1, 
  5}},ExpressionUUID->"c630b728-be9f-4945-860a-1d20ba936a23"],

Cell["\<\
The maximum value quantum mechanics itself allows for the CHSH combination S \
= E(a0,b0) + E(a0,b1) + E(a1,b0) \[Minus] E(a1,b1): whereas local \
hidden-variable theories obey |S| \[LessEqual] 2, every quantum state and \
choice of \[PlusMinus]1-valued measurements satisfies |S| \[LessEqual] 2\
\[Sqrt]2 \[TildeTilde] 2.828, and a maximally entangled qubit pair with \
suitably rotated settings attains this ceiling exactly (Tsirelson, 1980). The \
bound thus marks quantum theory's own limit, strictly between the classical \
bound 2 and the algebraic maximum 4 \[LongDash] the two-party analogue of the \
Lov\[AAcute]sz number \[CurlyTheta] sitting between \[Alpha] and \[Alpha]* in \
the essay's \[Alpha] \[LessEqual] \[CurlyTheta] \[LessEqual] \[Alpha]* \
hierarchy for the KCBS pentagon.\
\>", "Text",
 CellMargins->{{104, 30}, {4, 4}},
 FontSize->12,ExpressionUUID->"03b996b5-68de-8d43-a9be-a4bfe102f8f9"],

Cell["\<\
Build the state that makes this possible, as an actual circuit, using the \
Wolfram Quantum Computation Framework (loaded once per session; it \
auto-installs from the public paclet repository the first time):\
\>", "CodeText",ExpressionUUID->"88c27cf1-1e05-2842-8b42-68299e90d1be"],

Cell["Needs[\"Wolfram`QuantumFramework`\"]", "Input",
 CellOpen->False,ExpressionUUID->"bff287cf-8ab7-d04b-978e-4cda5e232162"],

Cell["\<\
A Hadamard, then a controlled-NOT, applied to two qubits starting at \
\[VerticalSeparator]00\[RightAngleBracket] \[LongDash] the standard Bell-pair \
preparation, drawn natively:\
\>", "CodeText",ExpressionUUID->"c83482c8-aa0f-b74a-abac-b79c1cef1308"],

