Cell[TextData[{
 StyleBox["\[FilledRightTriangle] ",
  FontSize->10,
  FontColor->GrayLevel[0.55]],
 StyleBox["quantum circuit (Hadamard and CNOT gates)",
  FontSize->11,
  FontWeight->"Bold",
  FontColor->GrayLevel[0.3]],
 StyleBox["  \[Bullet] QuantumCircuitOperator diagram, gate chain, H, \
controlled-NOT, 51-gate circuit, gate cascade",
  FontSize->10,
  FontColor->GrayLevel[0.45]]
}], "Text",
 ShowGroupOpener->True,
 CellMargins->{{86, 30}, {1, 
  5}},ExpressionUUID->"a39eec2f-fdd4-4745-85d8-24aac88d53d2"],

Cell["\<\
A quantum circuit is a sequence of unitary gates applied to wires, one per \
qubit (or qudit); the whole circuit is the matrix product of its gates, taken \
in reverse order of application, with identities filling the untouched wires. \
The Hadamard gate H = (1/\[Sqrt]2)\[CenterDot][[1, 1], [1, \[Minus]1]] sends \
|0\:27e9 to (|0\:27e9 + |1\:27e9)/\[Sqrt]2, an equal superposition. The \
controlled-NOT (CNOT) acts on two qubits, flipping the target exactly when \
the control is |1\:27e9: |c, t\:27e9 \[RightArrow] |c, t \[CirclePlus] \
c\:27e9. Applied to |00\:27e9, H on the control qubit followed by CNOT yields \
the entangled Bell state (|00\:27e9 + |11\:27e9)/\[Sqrt]2 \[LongDash] the \
essay's Bell-pair preparation \[LongDash] and the same circuit model carries \
its qutrit gate cascades, up to the 51-gate necklace circuit.\
\>", "Text",
 CellMargins->{{104, 30}, {4, 4}},
 FontSize->12,ExpressionUUID->"40ea3178-3ddb-c545-84a2-ac9fa2f06ada"],

Cell["\<\
Apply it, and check genuine entanglement directly \[LongDash] not just \
correlation, but a state that provably cannot be written as one qubit times \
another:\
\>", "CodeText",ExpressionUUID->"20eb1a03-09f7-8d4c-a252-d54731087db0"],

Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell["\<\
QuantumEntangledQ[QuantumCircuitOperator[{\"H\", \"CNOT\"}][QuantumState[{1, \
0, 0, 0}]]]\
\>", "Input",
 CellOpen->False,ExpressionUUID->"17ffaf63-8278-014a-b0fd-0ae59b03c35e"],

Cell[BoxData["True"], "Output",ExpressionUUID->"b27a29b8-1800-dd47-8b52-ea40d1d0f8b6"]
}, Open  ]],

Cell["\<\
Now read off the four correlators at the settings that saturate CHSH \
\[LongDash] Pauli Z and X for Alice, their \[PlusMinus]45\[Degree] rotations \
for Bob \[LongDash] first by direct linear algebra (Pauli matrices, one \
KroneckerProduct inner product per correlator), the ground truth every later \
route is checked against:\
\>", "CodeText",ExpressionUUID->"b002854b-be64-ab4b-9d9d-6cf10fd751ab"],

Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell["\<\
Module[{sz, sx, bell, a0, a1, b0, b1, corr}, sz = {{1, 0}, {0, -1}}; sx = \
{{0, 1}, {1, 0}}; bell = {1, 0, 0, 1}/Sqrt[2]; a0 = sz; a1 = sx; b0 = (sz + \
sx)/Sqrt[2]; b1 = (sz - sx)/Sqrt[2]; corr[a_, b_] := bell . \
KroneckerProduct[a, b] . bell; FullSimplify[{corr[a0, b0], corr[a0, b1], \
corr[a1, b0], corr[a1, b1]}]]\
\>", "Input",
 CellOpen->False,ExpressionUUID->"a76bf379-5c9b-fd47-8ebb-73d2a45b9ea7"],

Cell[BoxData[
 RowBox[{"{", 
  RowBox[{
   FractionBox["1", 
    SqrtBox["2"]], ",", 
   FractionBox["1", 
    SqrtBox["2"]], ",", 
   FractionBox["1", 
    SqrtBox["2"]], ",", 
   RowBox[{"-", 
    FractionBox["1", 
     SqrtBox["2"]]}]}], "}"}]], "Output",ExpressionUUID->"bffea0d0-7ec9-fc42-\
af11-d2af6eecce56"]
}, Open  ]],

Cell[TextData[{
 StyleBox["\[FilledRightTriangle] ",
  FontSize->10,
  FontColor->GrayLevel[0.55]],
 StyleBox["tensor (Kronecker) product",
  FontSize->11,
  FontWeight->"Bold",
  FontColor->GrayLevel[0.3]],
 StyleBox["  \[Bullet] KroneckerProduct, QuantumTensorProduct, joint \
observable, P_i \[CircleTimes] P_j, composite observable",
  FontSize->10,
  FontColor->GrayLevel[0.45]]
}], "Text",
 ShowGroupOpener->True,
 CellMargins->{{86, 30}, {1, 
  5}},ExpressionUUID->"a639159d-52f7-a54a-8ea2-cb243a911925"],

Cell["\<\
The composition rule for quantum subsystems: two systems with state spaces H\
\:2081 (dimension m) and H\:2082 (dimension n) combine into the \
mn-dimensional space H\:2081 \[CircleTimes] H\:2082, and operators A on H\
\:2081 and B on H\:2082 combine into A \[CircleTimes] B, defined by (A \
\[CircleTimes] B)(u \[CircleTimes] v) = Au \[CircleTimes] Bv. In matrix form \
this is the Kronecker product: the block matrix whose (i,j) block is a_ij\
\[CenterDot]B. A joint observable such as P_i \[CircleTimes] P_j then \
measures P_i on the first subsystem and P_j on the second simultaneously; on \
a product state its expectation factorizes, \:27e8u \[CircleTimes] v|A \
\[CircleTimes] B|u \[CircleTimes] v\:27e9 = \
\:27e8u|A|u\:27e9\:27e8v|B|v\:27e9. In the essay this is \
KroneckerProduct/QuantumTensorProduct \[LongDash] the \"independent \
products\" route that gluing is contrasted with.\
\>", "Text",
 CellMargins->{{104, 30}, {4, 4}},
 FontSize->12,ExpressionUUID->"6c9dec67-d74a-d847-b83b-9b170704ee2b"],

Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell["\<\
corrs = chshCorrelators[]; corrs[[1]] + corrs[[2]] + corrs[[3]] - corrs[[4]]\
\>", "Input",
 CellOpen->False,ExpressionUUID->"8d861382-0654-b545-8abf-7282e39a4cf7"],

Cell[BoxData[
 RowBox[{"2", " ", 
  SqrtBox["2"]}]], "Output",ExpressionUUID->"3d001bfe-acde-324d-a991-ba6a54c0a5e3"]
}, Open  ]],

Cell["\<\
The same four correlators can be computed end-to-end inside the framework \
itself \[LongDash] each setting pair composed into a joint observable with \
QuantumTensorProduct, wrapped in QuantumMeasurementOperator, and applied to \
the circuit-prepared Bell state. The EPR experiment runs as an actual \
quantum-computation pipeline \[LongDash] state preparation circuit, composite \
observable, measurement \[LongDash] and returns the same closed forms as the \
hand computation, checked symbol-for-symbol in the last entry:\
\>", "Text",ExpressionUUID->"c9558071-45eb-c146-a704-993052f1ad1a"],

Cell["\<\
Run the EPR/CHSH experiment natively \[LongDash] QuantumTensorProduct for the \
joint settings, QuantumMeasurementOperator for the statistics \[LongDash] and \
compare with the direct route:\
\>", "CodeText",ExpressionUUID->"32367c29-4e37-9743-b5cd-6c7bc26d45fa"],

Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell["\<\
Module[{bell, sz, sx, a0, a1, b0, b1, corrOf, corrs}, bell = \
QuantumCircuitOperator[{\"H\", \"CNOT\"}][QuantumState[{1, 0, 0, 0}]]; sz = \
{{1, 0}, {0, -1}}; sx = {{0, 1}, {1, 0}}; a0 = sz; a1 = sx; b0 = (sz + \
sx)/Sqrt[2]; b1 = (sz - sx)/Sqrt[2]; corrOf[a_, b_] := \
QuantumMeasurementOperator[QuantumTensorProduct[QuantumOperator[a, {2}, {1}], \
QuantumOperator[b, {2}, {2}]]][bell][\"Mean\"]; corrs = \
FullSimplify[{corrOf[a0, b0], corrOf[a0, b1], corrOf[a1, b0], corrOf[a1, \
b1]}]; {corrs, FullSimplify[corrs[[1]] + corrs[[2]] + corrs[[3]] - \
corrs[[4]]], corrs === chshCorrelators[]}]\
\>", "Input",
 CellOpen->False,ExpressionUUID->"169fba9e-348a-ae4d-ace5-7501987f4c6c"],

Cell[BoxData[
 RowBox[{"{", 
  RowBox[{
   RowBox[{"{", 
    RowBox[{
     FractionBox["1", 
      SqrtBox["2"]], ",", 
     FractionBox["1", 
      SqrtBox["2"]], ",", 
     FractionBox["1", 
      SqrtBox["2"]], ",", 
     RowBox[{"-", 
      FractionBox["1", 
       SqrtBox["2"]]}]}], "}"}], ",", 
   RowBox[{"2", " ", 
    SqrtBox["2"]}], ",", "True"}], "}"}]], "Output",ExpressionUUID->"a221cfdd-\
2ef9-224b-acf1-cb8b00227578"]
}, Open  ]],

Cell["\<\
The measurement statistics themselves, not just their sum, are the real \
content: three settings agree with probability cos^2(\[Pi]/8) \[TildeTilde] \
0.854 each (correlator +1/\[Sqrt]2), the fourth disagrees just as strongly \
(correlator -1/\[Sqrt]2) \[LongDash] and it is exactly this one sign flip, on \
top of three equal positive correlations, that pushes the sum past the \
classical ceiling of 2:\
\>", "CodeText",ExpressionUUID->"0b44a08f-f2e9-1c41-a1ee-1eaa65d0d7fa"],

Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell["\<\
BarChart[N[chshCorrelators[]], ChartLabels -> {\"E(a0,b0)\", \"E(a0,b1)\", \
\"E(a1,b0)\", \"E(a1,b1)\"}, ChartStyle -> RGBColor[0.2, 0.45, 0.85], \
GridLines -> {None, {-1, 1}}, GridLinesStyle -> GrayLevel[0.75], PlotRange -> \
{-1.15, 1.15}, ImageSize -> 420]\
\>", "Input",
 CellOpen->False,ExpressionUUID->"eee42e3a-21a2-e447-a01e-3528a5fe9464"],

