Cell["\<\
Recast as a game: a referee sends independent random bits x to Alice and y to \
Bob; each answers a bit without communicating; they win if their answers a, b \
satisfy a\[CirclePlus]b = x\[And]y, the AND of the questions. Every classical \
strategy \[LongDash] deterministic or using shared randomness \[LongDash] \
wins at most 3/4 of the time. Sharing an entangled pair instead lets Alice \
and Bob win with probability cos^2(\[Pi]/8) = (2+\[Sqrt]2)/4 \[TildeTilde] \
0.854 \[LongDash] a real, provable, experimentally confirmed edge with no \
classical explanation:\
\>", "Text",ExpressionUUID->"edf7ec29-30b8-224c-9b7b-a524a0eb9a92"],

Cell["Classical bound, quantum value, and the gap between them:", "CodeText",ExpressionUUID->"fedd3d4f-8604-d94e-a746-b760ce2f13ef"],

Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell["{3/4, FullSimplify[Cos[Pi/8]^2], N[Cos[Pi/8]^2 - 3/4, 4]}", "Input",
 CellOpen->False,ExpressionUUID->"3ca90409-1f2f-1a4f-97c0-dff2defbd985"],

Cell[BoxData[
 RowBox[{"{", 
  RowBox[{
   FractionBox["3", "4"], ",", 
   SuperscriptBox[
    RowBox[{"Cos", "[", 
     FractionBox["\[Pi]", "8"], "]"}], "2"], ",", 
   "0.1035533905932737621`4."}], "}"}]], "Output",ExpressionUUID->"72328ac5-\
e6f6-b349-ac1c-d6afb9eea2ef"]
}, Open  ]],

Cell["\<\
The resource behind both numbers is entanglement: Alice and Bob share a \
single quantum state, like the Bell pair (|00\[RightAngleBracket]+|11\
\[RightAngleBracket])/\[Sqrt]2 used above, that cannot be split into \
\[OpenCurlyDoubleQuote]system A in some state\[CloseCurlyDoubleQuote] and \
\[OpenCurlyDoubleQuote]system B in some state\[CloseCurlyDoubleQuote] \
separately. Measuring one half instantaneously fixes the statistics of \
measuring the other, in a way no classical joint distribution over \
predetermined outcomes can reproduce \[LongDash] yet without letting either \
party send the other an actual signal: no measurement Alice performs changes \
Bob's own local statistics, so nothing here permits faster-than-light \
communication.\
\>", "Text",ExpressionUUID->"4832f807-4f73-a14b-b944-9654873f1757"],

Cell[TextData[{
 StyleBox["\[FilledRightTriangle] ",
  FontSize->10,
  FontColor->GrayLevel[0.55]],
 StyleBox["no-signalling (post-quantum theories)",
  FontSize->11,
  FontWeight->"Bold",
  FontColor->GrayLevel[0.3]],
 StyleBox["  \[Bullet] no signaling, no faster-than-light communication, \
no-signalling ceiling, exclusivity-only theories, post-quantum contextuality, \
PR boxes",
  FontSize->10,
  FontColor->GrayLevel[0.45]]
}], "Text",
 ShowGroupOpener->True,
 CellMargins->{{86, 30}, {1, 
  5}},ExpressionUUID->"4ec84362-f909-0a4f-995b-4be8b769f529"],

Cell["\<\
A theory is no-signalling when one party's choice of measurement never alters \
another party's local statistics: for correlations p(a,b|x,y), the marginal \
\[CapitalSigma]_b p(a,b|x,y) is independent of the distant setting y, and \
symmetrically for x. Popescu and Rohrlich showed this axiom alone does not \
recover quantum mechanics \[LongDash] no-signalling \"PR boxes\" reach the \
CHSH value S = 4, beyond the quantum bound 2\[Sqrt]2. In contextuality the \
analogous minimal demand \[LongDash] exclusive events never both fire, \
nothing more \[LongDash] defines the post-quantum rung: on the KCBS pentagon, \
probability 1/2 on every event is allowed, giving the ceiling \[Alpha]*(C5) = \
5/2, strictly above the quantum \[CurlyTheta](C5) = \[Sqrt]5.\
\>", "Text",
 CellMargins->{{104, 30}, {4, 4}},
 FontSize->12,ExpressionUUID->"1a9d24ea-fd98-3146-950d-491331489cb3"],

Cell["\<\
CHSH needs two things at once: entanglement, and spatial separation \
\[LongDash] the two parties must be far enough apart that \
\[OpenCurlyDoubleQuote]no signaling\[CloseCurlyDoubleQuote] has real physical \
teeth. A natural question follows: is separation actually essential, or is \
entanglement doing all the work alone? Kochen and Specker (1967), and later \
Klyachko, Can, Binicioglu and Shumovsky (2008), showed the answer is no. A \
SINGLE quantum system \[LongDash] no entanglement, no separated parties, \
nothing but one qutrit and a sequence of measurements made on it \[LongDash] \
already forces the same kind of conclusion: no assignment of pre-existing \
values to measurement outcomes can reproduce quantum predictions, PROVIDED \
those values are required not to depend on which OTHER, compatible \
measurement happens to accompany them. This weaker, more general requirement \
\[LongDash] that an outcome shouldn't depend on its context, the other \
compatible measurements grouped with it \[LongDash] is called \
noncontextuality; its failure, contextuality, is strictly more general than \
nonlocality, of which Bell/CHSH is only the special spatially-separated case. \
The KCBS pentagon below is the smallest, sharpest example.\
\>", "Text",ExpressionUUID->"a1ee2148-7e62-004a-a344-e9757003dd1a"],

Cell[TextData[{
 StyleBox["\[FilledRightTriangle] ",
  FontSize->10,
  FontColor->GrayLevel[0.55]],
 StyleBox["noncontextuality / contextuality",
  FontSize->11,
  FontWeight->"Bold",
  FontColor->GrayLevel[0.3]],
 StyleBox["  \[Bullet] context-independence of outcomes, noncontextual \
hidden-variable model, noncontextual assignment of pre-existing \[PlusMinus]1 \
values, \[Alpha] bound",
  FontSize->10,
  FontColor->GrayLevel[0.45]]
}], "Text",
 ShowGroupOpener->True,
 CellMargins->{{86, 30}, {1, 
  5}},ExpressionUUID->"4f056372-5403-1040-a0ed-7b9b9819799e"],

Cell["\<\
Noncontextuality is the hypothesis that each measurement outcome reveals a \
pre-existing value that is independent of its context \[LongDash] the set of \
compatible measurements performed alongside it. Formally, a noncontextual \
hidden-variable model assigns every observable a definite value \
(\[PlusMinus]1 for the KCBS observables A_k = 1 \[Minus] \
2|v_k\:27e9\:27e8v_k|) by deterministic, context-independent assignments, \
together with arbitrary convex mixtures over such assignments. Every model in \
this class \[LongDash] mixtures included, which is why the bound covers all \
noncontextual models, not just deterministic ones \[LongDash] obeys \
\[CapitalSigma]\:27e8A_k A_(k+1)\:27e9 \[GreaterEqual] \[Minus]3 on the \
pentagon, equivalently \[CapitalSigma]p \[LessEqual] \[Alpha](C5) = 2 on the \
event ruler. Contextuality is the violation of such bounds; the qutrit \
reaches 5 \[Minus] 4\[Sqrt]5 \[TildeTilde] \[Minus]3.944.\
\>", "Text",
 CellMargins->{{104, 30}, {4, 4}},
 FontSize->12,ExpressionUUID->"104dd7ff-231e-ca45-b7d5-6639c1e740e4"],

Cell[TextData[{
 StyleBox["\[FilledRightTriangle] ",
  FontSize->10,
  FontColor->GrayLevel[0.55]],
 StyleBox["Kochen\[Dash]Specker theorem",
  FontSize->11,
  FontWeight->"Bold",
  FontColor->GrayLevel[0.3]],
 StyleBox["  \[Bullet] Kochen and Specker (1967), state-independent \
contextuality",
  FontSize->10,
  FontColor->GrayLevel[0.45]]
}], "Text",
 ShowGroupOpener->True,
 CellMargins->{{86, 30}, {1, 
  5}},ExpressionUUID->"68319f74-6c7d-9346-971f-3ecc4f2d4f9d"],

Cell["\<\
The theorem of Kochen and Specker (1967): on a Hilbert space of dimension \
\[GreaterEqual] 3, no assignment of definite values {0, 1} to all projection \
operators can satisfy the exclusivity-and-completeness rule that, in every \
set of mutually orthogonal projectors summing to the identity, exactly one \
receives the value 1 \[LongDash] provided each projector's value is \
noncontextual, i.e. independent of which compatible projectors it is measured \
alongside. The original proof exhibits a finite set of 117 directions in \
three dimensions admitting no such coloring. Unlike Bell/CHSH, the result is \
state-independent and needs no entanglement or spatial separation: a single \
qutrit suffices. KCBS is its state-dependent, experimentally testable \
descendant.\
\>", "Text",
 CellMargins->{{104, 30}, {4, 4}},
 FontSize->12,ExpressionUUID->"c2f1480d-b6f0-5845-8b6b-8e404bab706c"],

Cell[TextData[{
 StyleBox["\[FilledRightTriangle] ",
  FontSize->10,
  FontColor->GrayLevel[0.55]],
 StyleBox["measurement context / joint measurability (compatibility)",
  FontSize->11,
  FontWeight->"Bold",
  FontColor->GrayLevel[0.3]],
 StyleBox["  \[Bullet] context, compatible measurements, jointly measurable, \
commuting projectors, one context per pentagon edge, pairwise-compatible \
sharp measurements",
  FontSize->10,
  FontColor->GrayLevel[0.45]]
}], "Text",
 ShowGroupOpener->True,
 CellMargins->{{86, 30}, {1, 
  5}},ExpressionUUID->"e517ed17-218c-2644-89a8-11ef932f9dfb"],

Cell["\<\
Two quantum measurements are compatible (jointly measurable) when a single \
experimental arrangement can yield both outcomes at once; for sharp \
observables this holds exactly when they commute, [A, B] = AB \[Minus] BA = \
0. A measurement context is a set of mutually compatible observables measured \
together in one run \[LongDash] and for sharp measurements pairwise \
commutativity already guarantees that the whole set is jointly measurable. \
Noncontextuality demands that an outcome's value be independent of which \
context it is measured in. In the KCBS pentagon C5, adjacent directions are \
orthogonal, so each pair (A_k, A_(k+1)) commutes and forms one context per \
pentagon edge \[LongDash] five contexts, each sharing one observable with \
each neighbor.\
\>", "Text",
 CellMargins->{{104, 30}, {4, 4}},
 FontSize->12,ExpressionUUID->"3d678991-1859-8f41-b5e2-173aba9d9791"],

Cell[TextData[{
 StyleBox["\[FilledRightTriangle] ",
  FontSize->10,
  FontColor->GrayLevel[0.55]],
 StyleBox["quantum nonlocality",
  FontSize->11,
  FontWeight->"Bold",
  FontColor->GrayLevel[0.3]],
 StyleBox["  \[Bullet] nonlocality, spatially-separated case of contextuality",
  FontSize->10,
  FontColor->GrayLevel[0.45]]
}], "Text",
 ShowGroupOpener->True,
 CellMargins->{{86, 30}, {1, 
  5}},ExpressionUUID->"4664c7fb-b6b0-334c-9905-19bb615dde3c"],

Cell["\<\
Quantum nonlocality is the failure of correlations between spatially \
separated systems to admit any local hidden-variable model \[LongDash] a \
distribution over hidden states \[Lambda] in which each party's outcome \
depends only on \[Lambda] and its own local setting, so joint probabilities \
factorize. Bell's theorem shows entangled states violate bounds every such \
model obeys; in the CHSH form, S = E(a0,b0) + E(a0,b1) + E(a1,b0) \[Minus] \
E(a1,b1) satisfies |S| \[LessEqual] 2 for local models, while quantum \
mechanics reaches 2\[Sqrt]2. Nonlocality nonetheless respects no-signaling: \
neither party's local statistics depend on the other's setting. In this essay \
it is the special spatially separated case of contextuality.\
\>", "Text",
 CellMargins->{{104, 30}, {4, 4}},
 FontSize->12,ExpressionUUID->"62652413-a20d-7d4a-bea1-2b14cb0360cb"]
}, Open  ]],

Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell["The Atom: The KCBS Pentagon", "Section",ExpressionUUID->"109e2847-7d0a-874c-a3fb-c41ce1841802"],

Cell["\<\
The five-cycle exclusivity graph C5 \[LongDash] five measurement outcomes, \
each excluding its two neighbors \[LongDash] is the smallest object carrying \
the full classical / quantum / post-quantum hierarchy identified by Cabello, \
Severini and Winter: the independence number \[Alpha](C5) = 2 bounds any \
noncontextual hidden-variable model, the Lov\[AAcute]sz number \
\[CurlyTheta](C5) = \[Sqrt]5 \[LongDash] Lov\[AAcute]sz's own 1979 pentagon \
example, the result that introduced this graph invariant in the first place \
\[LongDash] bounds quantum mechanics, and the fractional packing number \
\[Alpha]*(C5) = 5/2 bounds any no-signalling (post-quantum) theory. Because 2 \
< \[Sqrt]5 < 5/2 strictly, this one pentagon already separates all three \
levels. Everything below is built by composing copies of this atom.\
\>", "Text",ExpressionUUID->"97012dcd-e03d-4f4d-b2b8-48795861e534"],

Cell[TextData[{
 StyleBox["\[FilledRightTriangle] ",
  FontSize->10,
  FontColor->GrayLevel[0.55]],
 StyleBox["Lov\[AAcute]sz number \[CurlyTheta](G)",
  FontSize->11,
  FontWeight->"Bold",
  FontColor->GrayLevel[0.3]],
 StyleBox["  \[Bullet] \[CurlyTheta](C5) = \[Sqrt]5, \[CurlyTheta](G_W), \
\[CurlyTheta]-bar(W), Lov\[AAcute]sz theta SDP, quantum bound, Lov\[AAcute]sz \
(1979)",
  FontSize->10,
  FontColor->GrayLevel[0.45]]
}], "Text",
 ShowGroupOpener->True,
 CellMargins->{{86, 30}, {1, 
  5}},ExpressionUUID->"317964d7-48e5-c140-a3c7-223cb20dccad"],

Cell["\<\
The Lov\[AAcute]sz number \[CurlyTheta](G) of a graph G is a \
semidefinite-programming invariant sandwiched between the independence number \
and the fractional packing number: \[Alpha](G) \[LessEqual] \[CurlyTheta](G) \
\[LessEqual] \[Alpha]*(G). It equals the maximum of the sum of all entries of \
a real symmetric positive-semidefinite matrix X with Tr X = 1 and X_ij = 0 \
for every edge ij; equivalently, the maximum of \[CapitalSigma]\:1d62 \
\:27e8c, u\:1d62\:27e9\.b2 over a unit \"handle\" vector c and unit vectors u\
\:1d62 assigned to vertices with u\:1d62 \[UpTee] u\:2c7c on every edge. For \
an exclusivity graph it is the quantum-mechanical maximum of the event sum \
\[CapitalSigma]p, and it is multiplicative under the co-normal (OR) product \
that combines independent copies. Lov\[AAcute]sz (1979) introduced it with \
the pentagon, computing \[CurlyTheta](C5) = \[Sqrt]5 and thereby settling the \
Shannon capacity \[CapitalTheta](C5) = \[Sqrt]5.\
\>", "Text",
 CellMargins->{{104, 30}, {4, 4}},
 FontSize->12,ExpressionUUID->"4a73d26e-5335-f14b-bd08-4cad541c9bc8"],

Cell[TextData[{
 StyleBox["\[FilledRightTriangle] ",
  FontSize->10,
  FontColor->GrayLevel[0.55]],
 StyleBox["exclusivity graph",
  FontSize->11,
  FontWeight->"Bold",
  FontColor->GrayLevel[0.3]],
 StyleBox["  \[Bullet] C5 exclusivity graph, G_W, exclusive events, \
exclusivity structure",
  FontSize->10,
  FontColor->GrayLevel[0.45]]
}], "Text",
 ShowGroupOpener->True,
 CellMargins->{{86, 30}, {1, 
  5}},ExpressionUUID->"282fd0d8-6d6d-634b-b557-51786542f2dd"],

Cell["\<\
The exclusivity graph of a measurement scenario has one vertex per \
measurement event and an edge joining each pair of mutually exclusive events \
\[LongDash] events jointly testable in a single run yet never both occurring \
(quantumly, orthogonal projectors). Its graph invariants bound the event sum \
\[CapitalSigma]p attainable in each physical theory: noncontextual \
hidden-variable models reach at most the independence number \[Alpha](G), \
quantum mechanics at most the Lov\[AAcute]sz number \[CurlyTheta](G), and any \
no-signalling theory at most the fractional packing number \[Alpha]*(G), with \
\[Alpha](G) \[LessEqual] \[CurlyTheta](G) \[LessEqual] \[Alpha]*(G). For the \
KCBS pentagon C5 these read 2 < \[Sqrt]5 < 5/2 \[LongDash] the smallest \
strict separation \[LongDash] and the essay's glued composites G_W are \
exclusivity graphs built by identifying shared detectors.\
\>", "Text",
 CellMargins->{{104, 30}, {4, 4}},
 FontSize->12,ExpressionUUID->"05c58b37-8476-7a4c-b5ad-d51ef129576b"],

Cell[TextData[{
 StyleBox["\[FilledRightTriangle] ",
  FontSize->10,
  FontColor->GrayLevel[0.55]],
 StyleBox["fractional packing number \[Alpha]*(G) (and fractional edge cover)",
  FontSize->11,
  FontWeight->"Bold",
  FontColor->GrayLevel[0.3]],
 StyleBox["  \[Bullet] \[Alpha]*(C5) = 5/2, fractional-packing polytope, \
no-signalling ceiling, weight-1/2 edge cover of value 3/2 per block, LP \
duality squeeze",
  FontSize->10,
  FontColor->GrayLevel[0.45]]
}], "Text",
 ShowGroupOpener->True,
 CellMargins->{{86, 30}, {1, 
  5}},ExpressionUUID->"8ebf73d0-6243-0445-93af-26dc1c8be4a9"],

Cell["\<\
The fractional packing number \[Alpha]*(G) of an exclusivity graph G is the \
value of a linear program: maximize \[CapitalSigma] x_i over vertex weights \
x_i \[GreaterEqual] 0 with \[CapitalSigma]_{i\[Element]C} x_i \[LessEqual] 1 \
on every clique C. It caps the event sum \[CapitalSigma]p in any \
no-signalling theory obeying only exclusivity, so \[Alpha](G) \[LessEqual] \
\[CurlyTheta](G) \[LessEqual] \[Alpha]*(G). On triangle-free graphs such as \
C5 the cliques are just edges, so the constraint reads x_i + x_j \[LessEqual] \
1, and weight 1/2 everywhere gives \[Alpha]*(C5) = 5/2. This edge-form LP has \
as its dual the fractional edge cover: minimize \[CapitalSigma] y_e over edge \
weights y_e \[GreaterEqual] 0 summing to at least 1 at every vertex. Weak \
duality puts every packing \[LessEqual] every cover, so matching values (3/2 \
per block) certify the value exactly.\
\>", "Text",
 CellMargins->{{104, 30}, {4, 4}},
 FontSize->12,ExpressionUUID->"bbeea51a-cf95-6145-a6c5-86554d691de3"],

Cell[TextData[{
 StyleBox["\[FilledRightTriangle] ",
  FontSize->10,
  FontColor->GrayLevel[0.55]],
 StyleBox["independence number \[Alpha](G)",
  FontSize->11,
  FontWeight->"Bold",
  FontColor->GrayLevel[0.3]],
 StyleBox["  \[Bullet] \[Alpha](C5) = 2, \[Alpha](G_W), \[Alpha]-bar(W) \
(density per block), classical bound, FindIndependentVertexSet",
  FontSize->10,
  FontColor->GrayLevel[0.45]]
}], "Text",
 ShowGroupOpener->True,
 CellMargins->{{86, 30}, {1, 
  5}},ExpressionUUID->"5079a628-4acc-8c41-8252-357ab3d00491"],

Cell["\<\
An independent set in a graph G is a set of vertices no two of which are \
joined by an edge; the independence number \[Alpha](G) is the size of a \
largest such set. In an exclusivity graph, whose vertices are measurement \
events and whose edges join mutually exclusive pairs, \[Alpha](G) counts the \
most events a deterministic 0/1 assignment can mark 1 with no two adjacent \
1s, so every noncontextual hidden-variable model obeys \[CapitalSigma]p \
\[LessEqual] \[Alpha](G). For the KCBS pentagon, \[Alpha](C5) = 2. The essay \
also uses the per-block density \[Alpha]-bar(W), the independence number \
divided by the number of glued pentagons.\
\>", "Text",
 CellMargins->{{104, 30}, {4, 4}},
 FontSize->12,ExpressionUUID->"04a72ec8-30fa-d342-9fb3-7dfd5ffb6a96"],

Cell["Build the C5 exclusivity graph:", "CodeText",ExpressionUUID->"0cc235b1-a6ce-8549-9081-80d98e04dada"],

