Cell[TextData[{
 "One wire, five gates: F1 rotates the input into context 1's own measurement \
basis; each ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  FormBox[
   SubscriptBox["T", "k"], TraditionalForm]],ExpressionUUID->
  "b214fa29-3a06-374c-af6e-a42b9dfecd76"],
 " then advances by exactly one incremental reconfiguration step. Applying \
the growing gate chain (F1; F1,T1; \[Ellipsis]; F1,T1,T2,T3,T4) to the \
pentagon's own symmetry axis and reading the resulting amplitude off the \
circuit's own matrix gives every context's click probability directly \
\[LongDash] a second, fully independent route to the same numbers, so the two \
pipelines confirm each other:"
}], "CodeText",ExpressionUUID->"fbf96a5c-cfa1-ce44-9d6d-0f27d88b6e2e"],

Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell["\<\
Module[{gates, circuits, handle, probs}, gates = lapkiewiczGates[]; circuits \
= Table[QuantumCircuitOperator[Take[gates, k + 1]], {k, 0, 4}]; handle = {0., \
0., 1.}; probs = Table[Abs[(circuits[[k + 1]][\"Matrix\"] . handle)[[1]]]^2, \
{k, 0, 4}]; {AllTrue[circuits, #1[\"UnitaryQ\"] & ], probs, Total[probs], \
N[Sqrt[5]]}]\
\>", "Input",
 CellOpen->False,ExpressionUUID->"c5ea83fd-5105-7549-a21e-830f81aa28d4"],

Cell[BoxData[
 RowBox[{"{", 
  RowBox[{"True", ",", 
   RowBox[{"{", 
    RowBox[{
    "0.4472135954999579`", ",", "0.4472135954999579`", ",", 
     "0.4472135954999579`", ",", "0.4472135954999579`", ",", 
     "0.4472135954999579`"}], "}"}], ",", "2.23606797749979`", ",", 
   "2.23606797749979`"}], "}"}]], "Output",ExpressionUUID->"fc49e2b5-1709-\
7c43-b7b1-ed05c07dbf98"]
}, Open  ]],

Cell[TextData[{
 StyleBox["\[FilledRightTriangle] ",
  FontSize->10,
  FontColor->GrayLevel[0.55]],
 StyleBox["unitary operator / unitarity",
  FontSize->11,
  FontWeight->"Bold",
  FontColor->GrayLevel[0.3]],
 StyleBox["  \[Bullet] UnitaryQ, 'confirmed unitary', norm-preserving \
evolution, reversibility",
  FontSize->10,
  FontColor->GrayLevel[0.45]]
}], "Text",
 ShowGroupOpener->True,
 CellMargins->{{86, 30}, {1, 
  5}},ExpressionUUID->"c43a5558-890b-b245-b1dd-532581c8baba"],

Cell["\<\
A unitary operator U on a Hilbert space is a linear operator whose conjugate \
transpose is its inverse: U\[Dagger]U = UU\[Dagger] = I. Unitarity is exactly \
the condition that U preserves inner products, hence norms \[LongDash] so \
quantum state vectors stay normalized and outcome probabilities always sum to \
1 \[LongDash] and that the evolution is reversible, since U\[Dagger] undoes \
U. Quantum mechanics postulates that every closed-system evolution, and thus \
every quantum gate and circuit, is unitary. In this essay, UnitaryQ \
\[RightArrow] True is the machine check that a constructed gate satisfies U\
\[Dagger]U = I; both direct (det +1) and twisted (det \[Minus]1) gluing gates \
pass it, since unitarity fixes only |det U| = 1.\
\>", "Text",
 CellMargins->{{104, 30}, {4, 4}},
 FontSize->12,ExpressionUUID->"3bbc5347-d5b6-ec4d-bf29-664f301b0ba0"],

Cell[TextData[{
 "All five circuits are unitary, and all five give the same probability, ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  FormBox[
   FractionBox["1", 
    SqrtBox["5"]], TraditionalForm]],ExpressionUUID->
  "695341f1-585a-314a-966d-619987bf89c2"],
 ", exactly \[LongDash] the pentagon's own five-fold symmetry guarantees \
every context is equivalent, and the gate cascade respects that exactly \
rather than approximately, summing to ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  FormBox[
   RowBox[{
    RowBox[{"\[CurlyTheta]", "(", 
     SubscriptBox["C", "5"], ")"}], "=", 
    SqrtBox["5"]}], TraditionalForm]],ExpressionUUID->
  "d4f4bb71-a860-2449-a5b7-e52fac249d70"],
 " across all five settings."
}], "Text",ExpressionUUID->"e4409d55-2403-eb46-bd23-3bfd9917fcec"],

Cell["\<\
The same bound \[CurlyTheta](C5) = \[Sqrt]5 has a second derivation that \
anticipates the rest of this essay: take two independent copies of the \
pentagon. Their 25 joint events form the exclusivity graph built from the \
co-normal (OR) product of the two C5's \[LongDash] the same composition \
operation named again in the next section, but applied here to independent \
copies rather than glued neighbors. Quantum mechanics saturates it at \
\[CurlyTheta] = 5 exactly, and Lov\[AAcute]sz-number multiplicativity under \
the OR product forces the single-copy value back down to \[CurlyTheta](C5) = \
\[Sqrt]5. The two ways of combining pentagons this essay is built around \
\[LongDash] independent copies and edge-gluing \[LongDash] already show up, \
in miniature, inside the atom's own quantum bound.\
\>", "Text",ExpressionUUID->"5753e42e-3a82-544d-a115-ed5796fa2c66"],

Cell[TextData[{
 StyleBox["\[FilledRightTriangle] ",
  FontSize->10,
  FontColor->GrayLevel[0.55]],
 StyleBox["co-normal (OR) graph product",
  FontSize->11,
  FontWeight->"Bold",
  FontColor->GrayLevel[0.3]],
 StyleBox["  \[Bullet] OR product, disjunctive product, independent-copies \
composition, \[CurlyTheta]-multiplicativity under the OR product",
  FontSize->10,
  FontColor->GrayLevel[0.45]]
}], "Text",
 ShowGroupOpener->True,
 CellMargins->{{86, 30}, {1, 
  5}},ExpressionUUID->"70733a09-ae5b-a94d-93c9-785dff29f767"],

Cell["\<\
The co-normal (OR) product G\[Or]H of two graphs has vertex set \
V(G)\[Times]V(H), with (u\:2081,u\:2082) adjacent to (v\:2081,v\:2082) \
exactly when u\:2081~v\:2081 in G or u\:2082~v\:2082 in H. For exclusivity \
graphs this is the natural composition of independent experiments: a pair of \
joint events is exclusive as soon as either component pair is. The Lov\
\[AAcute]sz number is multiplicative under this product, \
\[CurlyTheta](G\[Or]H) = \[CurlyTheta](G)\[CenterDot]\[CurlyTheta](H) \
\[LongDash] the complement-form of Lov\[AAcute]sz's strong-product identity, \
proved in the same 1979 paper. Applied to two independent KCBS pentagons, the \
25-event graph C\:2085\[Or]C\:2085 has quantum value \[CurlyTheta] = 5, and \
multiplicativity forces the single-pentagon bound \[CurlyTheta](C\:2085) = \
\[Sqrt]5 \[LongDash] the essay's second derivation of the KCBS quantum \
maximum.\
\>", "Text",
 CellMargins->{{104, 30}, {4, 4}},
 FontSize->12,ExpressionUUID->"8ad315c9-9b0a-ec47-9428-ec95894e0955"],

Cell[TextData[{
 "That saturation is measurable, and measured natively: on the doubled handle \
state, every one of the 25 joint projectors ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  FormBox[
   RowBox[{
    SubscriptBox["P", "i"], "\[CircleTimes]", 
    SubscriptBox["P", "j"]}], TraditionalForm]],ExpressionUUID->
  "21b45d1b-88fa-e746-9c87-515f48bfbd1f"],
 " clicks with probability exactly ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  FormBox[
   RowBox[{
    SuperscriptBox[
     RowBox[{"(", 
      FractionBox["1", 
       SqrtBox["5"]], ")"}], "2"], "=", 
    FractionBox["1", "5"]}], TraditionalForm]],ExpressionUUID->
  "e5527b59-ba97-634c-bd88-facd042c1153"],
 ", and the 25 of them sum to exactly 5 \[LongDash] measuring composite \
observables like ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  FormBox[
   RowBox[{
    SubscriptBox["P", "i"], "\[CircleTimes]", 
    SubscriptBox["P", "j"]}], TraditionalForm]],ExpressionUUID->
  "97ae5ddd-5ae5-c343-a42d-2e89eaa59342"],
 " through QuantumMeasurementOperator, the very computation this composition \
story runs on:"
}], "Text",ExpressionUUID->"cc47201c-fdba-114f-8bff-aed35736cc09"],

Cell["\<\
Measure all 25 tensor-product projectors of two independent pentagons on the \
doubled handle state \[LongDash] one distinct value, 1/5, and the saturating \
total:\
\>", "CodeText",ExpressionUUID->"ee76991a-06a1-bc4e-ae79-32241f66feb0"],

Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell["\<\
Module[{c2, vecs, projs, handle2, mean, means25}, c2 = Cos[Pi/5]/(1 + \
Cos[Pi/5]); vecs = N[Table[{Sqrt[1 - c2]*Cos[4*Pi*(i/5)], Sqrt[1 - \
c2]*Sin[4*Pi*(i/5)], Sqrt[c2]}, {i, 0, 4}], 20]; projs = (Outer[Times, #1, \
#1] & ) /@ vecs; handle2 = QuantumState[Flatten[KroneckerProduct[{0, 0, 1}, \
{0, 0, 1}]], {3, 3}]; mean[i_, j_] := \
QuantumMeasurementOperator[QuantumTensorProduct[QuantumOperator[projs[[i]], \
{3}, {1}], QuantumOperator[projs[[j]], {3}, {2}]]][handle2][\"Mean\"]; \
means25 = Flatten[Table[mean[i, j], {i, 5}, {j, 5}]]; \
{DeleteDuplicates[Round[means25, 10^(-10)]], Total[means25]}]\
\>", "Input",
 CellOpen->False,ExpressionUUID->"51720f63-c835-bf4f-b0c1-f7e08357044c"],

Cell[BoxData[
 RowBox[{"{", 
  RowBox[{
   RowBox[{"{", 
    FractionBox["1", "5"], "}"}], ",", 
   "5.00000000000000000053281769708349461879`12.225959824339931"}], 
  "}"}]], "Output",ExpressionUUID->"f75df96c-c82a-0049-8f97-e4d7fef89062"]
}, Open  ]]
}, Open  ]],

Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell["Composition by Gluing: Direct and Twisted", "Section",ExpressionUUID->"db87820c-ed02-8b45-af24-053646fb6212"],

Cell["\<\
There are two distinct ways to compose several pentagons into a larger \
scenario. One is to take independent copies and combine them with the \
co-normal (OR) product of their exclusivity graphs \[LongDash] a route this \
project studies separately. The other, below, is graph surgery: literally \
sharing a detector between neighboring pentagons, gluing them edge-to-edge \
into a chain or a ring. At every shared joint there are exactly two ways to \
attach the next pentagon, called direct and twisted \[LongDash] named for \
what each does to the pentagon's own cyclic vertex order: direct advances it \
by a rotation, twisted reverses it by a reflection. Both are genuine elements \
of the pentagon's symmetry group, built from and verified directly against \
the KCBS vectors v_k introduced above:\
\>", "Text",ExpressionUUID->"e6cf0da8-f1a7-494b-bca7-04996412978f"],

Cell[TextData[{
 StyleBox["\[FilledRightTriangle] ",
  FontSize->10,
  FontColor->GrayLevel[0.55]],
 StyleBox["orthogonal group O(3): isometries, rotations vs reflections (det = \
\[PlusMinus]1)",
  FontSize->11,
  FontWeight->"Bold",
  FontColor->GrayLevel[0.3]],
 StyleBox["  \[Bullet] rigid attachment, isometry of real three-dimensional \
space, orientation-preserving/reversing, R_twisted = diag(1,1,-1).R_direct, \
(-1)^#twisted, determinant multiplicativity",
  FontSize->10,
  FontColor->GrayLevel[0.45]]
}], "Text",
 ShowGroupOpener->True,
 CellMargins->{{86, 30}, {1, 
  5}},ExpressionUUID->"76b01409-b837-2943-a464-bc0cfbec74b3"],

Cell["\<\
The orthogonal group O(3) is the group of all linear maps R of real \
three-dimensional space that preserve lengths and angles \[LongDash] \
equivalently, all 3\[Times]3 real matrices satisfying R\:1d40R = I. These are \
exactly the origin-fixing isometries of R\.b3, so every rigid attachment of \
one pentagon frame to another is an element of O(3). Taking determinants of R\
\:1d40R = I gives det R = \[PlusMinus]1, splitting O(3) into its two \
connected components: the rotations (det = +1, the gluing letter d) and the \
orientation-reversing maps (det = \[Minus]1) \[LongDash] reflections and \
rotoreflections \[LongDash] among them the letter t's reflection, with \
R_twisted = diag(1,1,\[Minus]1)\[CenterDot]R_direct exhibiting the sign flip. \
Since det(R\:2081R\:2082) = det R\:2081 \[CenterDot] det R\:2082, any \
composed gluing word carries the well-defined parity (\[Minus]1)^#twisted.\
\>", "Text",
 CellMargins->{{104, 30}, {4, 4}},
 FontSize->12,ExpressionUUID->"9dc49207-c6d9-844a-8d37-32806a19719b"],

Cell["\<\
Apply each joint type to the pentagon's own vertex labels: direct rotates \
0->1->2->3->4->0 (determinant +1); twisted reflects across the axis through \
vertex 2, fixing it and swapping 0 with 4, 1 with 3 (determinant -1):\
\>", "CodeText",ExpressionUUID->"64b9bb6e-59f3-4f45-8188-2d15780479d1"],

Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell["\<\
Module[{coral, grayN, grayLine, teal, ink, inkSub, pentAngles, Rp, nodeRp, \
pentUnit, directC, twistedC, directPos, twistedPos, trimSeg, directArrows, \
directNodes, directLabels, twistedOutline, axisLine, swapPair, swap04, \
swap13, twistedNodes, twistedLabels, titles}, coral = RGBColor[0.6, 0.24, \
0.11]; grayN = RGBColor[0.27, 0.27, 0.25]; grayLine = RGBColor[0.78, 0.77, \
0.73]; teal = RGBColor[0.06, 0.43, 0.34]; ink = GrayLevel[0.15]; inkSub = \
GrayLevel[0.42]; pentAngles = Table[(90 - 72*k)*Degree, {k, 0, 4}]; Rp = 2.1; \
nodeRp = 0.24; pentUnit = Table[Rp*{Cos[a], Sin[a]}, {a, pentAngles}]; \
directC = {-3.3, 0}; twistedC = {3.3, 0}; directPos = (directC + #1 & ) /@ \
pentUnit; twistedPos = (twistedC + #1 & ) /@ pentUnit; trimSeg[a_, b_, \
gap_:0.3] := Module[{u = (b - a)/Norm[b - a]}, {a + gap*u, b - gap*u}]; \
directArrows = Table[{teal, Thickness[0.0048], Arrowheads[0.032], \
Arrow[trimSeg[directPos[[k + 1]], directPos[[Mod[k + 1, 5] + 1]]]]}, {k, 0, \
4}]; directNodes = Table[{grayN, Disk[directPos[[k + 1]], nodeRp]}, {k, 0, \
4}]; directLabels = Table[Text[Style[ToString[k], 15, White, Bold], \
directPos[[k + 1]]], {k, 0, 4}]; twistedOutline = Table[{grayLine, \
Thickness[0.0022], Line[trimSeg[twistedPos[[k + 1]], twistedPos[[Mod[k + 1, \
5] + 1]], 0.26]]}, {k, 0, 4}]; axisLine = {GrayLevel[0.62], \
Thickness[0.0018], Dashing[{0.02, 0.015}], Line[{twistedPos[[3]], \
Mean[{twistedPos[[5]], twistedPos[[1]]}]}]}; swapPair[a_, b_] := Module[{mid \
= Mean[{a, b}], u}, u = (b - a)/Norm[b - a]; {coral, Thickness[0.0048], \
Arrowheads[0.032], Arrow[{mid, b - 0.3*u}], Arrow[{mid, a + 0.3*u}]}]; swap04 \
= swapPair[twistedPos[[1]], twistedPos[[5]]]; swap13 = \
swapPair[twistedPos[[2]], twistedPos[[4]]]; twistedNodes = Table[{grayN, \
Disk[twistedPos[[k + 1]], nodeRp]}, {k, 0, 4}]; twistedLabels = \
Table[Text[Style[ToString[k], 15, White, Bold], twistedPos[[k + 1]]], {k, 0, \
4}]; titles = {Text[Style[\"direct\", 19, ink], directC + {0, -2.25}], \
Text[Style[\"rotation, det = +1\", 13, inkSub], directC + {0, -2.65}], \
Text[Style[\"twisted\", 19, ink], twistedC + {0, -2.25}], \
Text[Style[\"reflection, det = -1\", 13, inkSub], twistedC + {0, -2.65}]}; \
Graphics[{directArrows, directNodes, directLabels, twistedOutline, axisLine, \
swap04, swap13, twistedNodes, twistedLabels, titles}, PlotRange -> {{-5.9, \
5.9}, {-3., 2.4}}, ImageSize -> 560, Background -> White]]\
\>", "Input",
 CellOpen->False,ExpressionUUID->"db2e0802-e6f5-c44d-8a67-453c8d83f3bc"],

