  PlotLabel->FormBox[
   "\"a ddt necklace at scale: 51 pentagon blocks glued into a closed ring \
(153 detectors, 204 exclusivities); teal blocks mark an odd cumulative \
twisted-count -- a genuine reflection, not a decorative rotation\"", 
    TraditionalForm],
  PlotRange->All]], "Output",ExpressionUUID->"05ceeb26-3687-f74b-b0ae-\
be46caac97a4"]
}, Open  ]],

Cell["\<\
A 180-degree rotation and a reflection are not the same operation \[LongDash] \
one preserves orientation (det = +1), the other reverses it (det = -1) \
\[LongDash] so the mirrored (teal) blocks above are drawn as actual mirror \
images, not rotated copies. That mirror pattern is the sign of an accumulated \
matrix, not an artistic choice: composing R_direct or R_twisted once per \
joint from block 0 up to block k gives a matrix M_k, and the block is \
mirrored exactly when Det[M_k] = -1. Confirmed three independent ways at \
60-digit precision \[LongDash] the raw 51-fold matrix product; that same \
product's exact factorization as the already-verified 3-gate (ddt) unit cell \
(the circuit two subsections above) raised to the 17th power; and an actual \
51-gate QuantumCircuitOperator built from real qutrit gates, through the \
Wolfram Quantum Computation Framework:\
\>", "CodeText",ExpressionUUID->"68a92606-500d-b240-91df-a8492f98bf55"],

Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell["\<\
Module[{letters, Rdirect, Rtwisted, gateOf, unitCell, gates, circuit, \
rawProduct}, letters = Characters[StringRepeat[\"ddt\", 17]]; Rdirect = \
N[RotationMatrix[4*(Pi/5), {1, 0, 0}], 60]; Rtwisted = DiagonalMatrix[{1, 1, \
-1}] . Rdirect; gateOf[l_] := If[l === \"d\", Rdirect, Rtwisted]; rawProduct \
= Fold[gateOf[#2] . #1 & , IdentityMatrix[3], letters]; unitCell = Rtwisted . \
Rdirect . Rdirect; gates = (QuantumOperator[gateOf[#1], 3] & ) /@ letters; \
circuit = QuantumCircuitOperator[gates]; {circuit[\"UnitaryQ\"], \
Chop[Det[circuit[\"Matrix\"]] + 1, 10^(-25)] == 0, Chop[MatrixPower[unitCell, \
17] - rawProduct, 10^(-25)] == ConstantArray[0, {3, 3}], \
Chop[circuit[\"Matrix\"] - rawProduct, 10^(-25)] == ConstantArray[0, {3, 3}]}]\
\>", "Input",
 CellOpen->False,ExpressionUUID->"6f93383f-5d4d-4d48-bd1d-5545f0935e21"],

Cell[BoxData[
 RowBox[{"{", 
  RowBox[{"True", ",", "True", ",", "True", ",", "True"}], "}"}]], "Output",Ex\
pressionUUID->"ec7f6d94-31f2-b446-9699-ba5d239bf115"]
}, Open  ]],

Cell[TextData[{
 StyleBox["\[FilledRightTriangle] ",
  FontSize->10,
  FontColor->GrayLevel[0.55]],
 StyleBox["first Stiefel\[Dash]Whitney class w1 (orientability; cylinder vs M\
\[ODoubleDot]bius)",
  FontSize->11,
  FontWeight->"Bold",
  FontColor->GrayLevel[0.3]],
 StyleBox["  \[Bullet] w_1 \[Element] H^1(ring; Z2), 'the complete \
topological invariant of the gluing pattern', (-1)^#twisted as a cohomology \
class, trivial vs nontrivial class, M\[ODoubleDot]bius band's single boundary \
loop",
  FontSize->10,
  FontColor->GrayLevel[0.45]]
}], "Text",
 ShowGroupOpener->True,
 CellMargins->{{86, 30}, {1, 
  5}},ExpressionUUID->"89c5eccb-6f1d-6646-84c2-ea4424ff2bf9"],

Cell["\<\
The first Stiefel\[Dash]Whitney class w1 of a real vector bundle is a \
cohomology class in H^1(base; Z2) that measures the obstruction to orienting \
the bundle: w1 = 0 exactly when a consistent orientation of the fibers \
exists. Concretely, it records whether the transition maps between local \
trivializations preserve or reverse orientation, so around any loop it \
evaluates to the sign of the holonomy determinant \[LongDash] here \
(\[Minus]1)^#twisted, the parity of t-joints in the gluing word. Line \
(interval) bundles over the circle are classified completely by w1: the \
trivial class gives the cylinder (two boundary loops), the nontrivial class \
the M\[ODoubleDot]bius band (one boundary loop) \[LongDash] the essay's \
cylinder/M\[ODoubleDot]bius dichotomy for glued pentagon rings.\
\>", "Text",
 CellMargins->{{104, 30}, {4, 4}},
 FontSize->12,ExpressionUUID->"37f4acec-c5ad-194b-9c13-c42497b5b661"],

Cell["\<\
All four checks hold: the 51-gate circuit is unitary, its net determinant is \
exactly -1 (17 twisted joints, an odd number), and both independent \
cross-checks match the raw product exactly \[LongDash] the drawn necklace, \
the algebraic unit-cell power, and the actual quantum circuit are three views \
of the same computation, not three separate claims.\
\>", "Text",ExpressionUUID->"2999a279-cba3-2941-aa5c-e17d611ef4ad"],

Cell["\<\
The necklace computation just verified has a classical name, and the name \
organizes everything this section has built. Label each joint of the base \
ring with its matrix \[LongDash] R_direct or R_twisted: in combinatorics a \
group-labeled graph is a voltage (gain) graph; in geometry the same data is a \
flat O(3)-bundle over the ring, presented by its gluing data \[LongDash] the \
descent formalism that assembles a manifold from charts or a gauge field from \
local trivializations (in physics dialect: a Z_2 lattice gauge field, whose \
Wilson loop is exactly the product above). The accumulated matrix around the \
ring \[LongDash] the 51-fold product \[LongDash] is the bundle's holonomy, \
and its determinant sign (-1)^#twisted is the complete topological invariant \
of the gluing pattern: the first Stiefel\[Dash]Whitney class w_1 in H^1(ring; \
Z_2). Even twisted-count is the trivial class \[LongDash] the cylinder; odd \
is the nontrivial one \[LongDash] the M\[ODoubleDot]bius band. The dichotomy \
is not an analogy: delete the glue rails from the glued graph itself and the \
surviving strands close into two separate loops exactly when the \
twisted-count is even (for the pure-direct ring they are precisely the C_N \
and C_2N of the theorem above), and into the M\[ODoubleDot]bius band's single \
boundary loop exactly when it is odd. Two facts keep the framing honest. \
First, the gauge moves of the Z_2 data \[LongDash] re-orienting one block's \
local frame, which flips the parity label at its two adjacent joints \
\[LongDash] collapse all 2^L parity words of a given length into exactly two \
classes, so at the level of bundle topology the parity is all that survives \
of a gluing word. Second, the Lov\[AAcute]sz densities are not functions of \
that class: ddt and pure twisted lie in the same M\[ODoubleDot]bius class yet \
have different gap densities. Topology decides orientability; the \
optimization problem of the closing sections lives in the finer geometry \
above it \[LongDash] one more way of seeing why no coarse invariant settles \
it:\
\>", "Text",ExpressionUUID->"962099f1-de48-f542-867c-c49826b72feb"],

Cell[TextData[{
 StyleBox["\[FilledRightTriangle] ",
  FontSize->10,
  FontColor->GrayLevel[0.55]],
 StyleBox["cohomology H^1(ring; Z2) and coboundaries",
  FontSize->11,
  FontWeight->"Bold",
  FontColor->GrayLevel[0.3]],
 StyleBox["  \[Bullet] H^1(C6; Z2), cohomology count, coboundary flips at \
every block, two gauge classes among 64 parity words, H^1 = Z^1/B^1",
  FontSize->10,
  FontColor->GrayLevel[0.45]]
}], "Text",
 ShowGroupOpener->True,
 CellMargins->{{86, 30}, {1, 
  5}},ExpressionUUID->"dd6610c2-f54d-934a-b1a2-48bd2f9e36b0"],

Cell["\<\
On the ring graph C_L (blocks as vertices, joints as edges), a Z2-valued \
1-cochain assigns each edge a parity label: 0 for direct, 1 for twisted. A \
0-cochain assigns a bit to each vertex; its coboundary \[Delta]s flips the \
labels of the two edges meeting every vertex where s = 1 \[LongDash] exactly \
the re-orientation of one block's frame. With no 2-cells, every 1-cochain is \
a cocycle, so H^1(C_L; Z2) = Z^1/B^1 \[TildeFullEqual] Z2: the 2^L parity \
words collapse under coboundary flips into exactly two classes, distinguished \
by the total parity \[CapitalSigma]w mod 2, i.e. the holonomy sign \
(\[Minus]1)^#twisted. Cell 145 verifies this by direct count for L = 6: 64 \
words, two classes.\
\>", "Text",
 CellMargins->{{104, 30}, {4, 4}},
 FontSize->12,ExpressionUUID->"e11b51d1-cf03-8d41-9cf6-5cc6ee47b8c1"],

Cell[TextData[{
 StyleBox["\[FilledRightTriangle] ",
  FontSize->10,
  FontColor->GrayLevel[0.55]],
 StyleBox["flat O(3)-bundle (gluing data / transition functions)",
  FontSize->11,
  FontWeight->"Bold",
  FontColor->GrayLevel[0.3]],
 StyleBox["  \[Bullet] flat bundle over the ring, descent formalism, charts, \
local trivializations, 'a gauge field from local trivializations'",
  FontSize->10,
  FontColor->GrayLevel[0.45]]
}], "Text",
 ShowGroupOpener->True,
 CellMargins->{{86, 30}, {1, 
  5}},ExpressionUUID->"e066c9b7-47a9-bf49-b18c-3a2385bd202c"],

Cell["\<\
A fibre bundle over a base covered by charts U\:1d62 is presented by its \
gluing data: transition functions g\:1d62\:2c7c on overlaps U\:1d62\:2229U\
\:2c7c, valued in the structure group G and satisfying the cocycle condition \
g\:1d62\:2096 = g\:1d62\:2c7c\[CenterDot]g\:2c7c\:2096 on triple overlaps, \
which glue the local trivializations U\:1d62\[Times]F into one total space. \
The bundle is flat when the g\:1d62\:2c7c can be chosen locally constant; \
flat G-bundles are then classified, up to gauge equivalence, by holonomy \
homomorphisms \[Pi]\:2081(base) \[RightArrow] G, taken up to conjugation in \
G. In the essay each joint's R_direct or R_twisted matrix is exactly such a \
transition function for a flat O(3)-bundle over the ring, whose holonomy \
homomorphism Z \[RightArrow] O(3) sends 1 to the gluing word's ordered \
product.\
\>", "Text",
 CellMargins->{{104, 30}, {4, 4}},
 FontSize->12,ExpressionUUID->"d1e0df7d-4290-a84b-822e-cd4ecbf4db8a"],

Cell[TextData[{
 StyleBox["\[FilledRightTriangle] ",
  FontSize->10,
  FontColor->GrayLevel[0.55]],
 StyleBox["gauge transformation / Z2 lattice gauge field",
  FontSize->11,
  FontWeight->"Bold",
  FontColor->GrayLevel[0.3]],
 StyleBox["  \[Bullet] gauge moves, gauge equivalence classes, re-orienting \
one block's local frame, parity word as link variables, Ising gauge model",
  FontSize->10,
  FontColor->GrayLevel[0.45]]
}], "Text",
 ShowGroupOpener->True,
 CellMargins->{{86, 30}, {1, 
  5}},ExpressionUUID->"fc592b8f-2544-5e4b-8c11-f8cbd3da66f2"],

Cell["\<\
A Z2 lattice gauge field assigns to each link \[ScriptL] of a graph a \
variable \[Sigma]\[ScriptL] \[Element] {+1, \[Minus]1}; in the essay the \
gluing word's parity labels (d \[RightTeeArrow] +1, t \[RightTeeArrow] \
\[Minus]1) play this role on the ring of pentagon blocks. A gauge \
transformation chooses a sign gv = \[PlusMinus]1 at each vertex and replaces \
\[Sigma]uv by gu \[Sigma]uv gv \[LongDash] concretely, re-orienting one \
block's local frame flips the parity label at its two adjacent joints. \
Products of link variables around closed loops (Wilson loops) are \
gauge-invariant, and on a single ring the loop parity (\[Minus]1)^#t is the \
only invariant: all 2^L parity words of length L collapse into exactly two \
gauge equivalence classes.\
\>", "Text",
 CellMargins->{{104, 30}, {4, 4}},
 FontSize->12,ExpressionUUID->"c2a87f0e-4253-424a-a304-57e50132fdaa"],

Cell[TextData[{
 StyleBox["\[FilledRightTriangle] ",
  FontSize->10,
  FontColor->GrayLevel[0.55]],
 StyleBox["holonomy (Wilson loop)",
  FontSize->11,
  FontWeight->"Bold",
  FontColor->GrayLevel[0.3]],
 StyleBox["  \[Bullet] the accumulated matrix around the ring, the 51-fold \
product, holonomy parity, ordered product of link matrices around a closed \
loop",
  FontSize->10,
  FontColor->GrayLevel[0.45]]
}], "Text",
 ShowGroupOpener->True,
 CellMargins->{{86, 30}, {1, 
  5}},ExpressionUUID->"0692c024-7e95-f44c-a686-2f0a19770acd"],

Cell["\<\
Holonomy is the net transformation produced by parallel transport around a \
closed loop: for a bundle or lattice gauge field given by link matrices g_1, \
\[Ellipsis], g_L along the loop's edges, it is the ordered product g_L \
\[CenterEllipsis] g_2 g_1 \[LongDash] in this essay, the 51-fold product of \
R_direct and R_twisted around the necklace. Changing the local frame at a \
site (a gauge move) only conjugates this product, so its \
conjugation-invariant content is what is physically meaningful; Wilson's loop \
observable, the trace of the ordered product of link variables around a \
closed lattice path, is the basic gauge-invariant quantity built from it. \
Here that invariant is the determinant sign (\[Minus]1)^#twisted, the parity \
separating cylinder from M\[ODoubleDot]bius gluings.\
\>", "Text",
 CellMargins->{{104, 30}, {4, 4}},
 FontSize->12,ExpressionUUID->"a09c7367-4864-824f-bf45-16111c845356"],

Cell[TextData[{
 StyleBox["\[FilledRightTriangle] ",
  FontSize->10,
  FontColor->GrayLevel[0.55]],
 StyleBox["voltage (gain) graph",
  FontSize->11,
  FontWeight->"Bold",
  FontColor->GrayLevel[0.3]],
 StyleBox["  \[Bullet] group-labeled graph, Z2-labeled ring, gluing data as \
edge labels",
  FontSize->10,
  FontColor->GrayLevel[0.45]]
}], "Text",
 ShowGroupOpener->True,
 CellMargins->{{86, 30}, {1, 
  5}},ExpressionUUID->"de8ca141-c744-034b-aecf-5c76cf4881ef"],

Cell["\<\
A voltage (gain) graph is a graph each of whose oriented edges carries an \
element of a group G; traversing an edge against its orientation contributes \
the inverse element, so every walk acquires a net group value \[LongDash] the \
ordered product of its edge labels \[LongDash] and every closed loop acquires \
a holonomy. Equivalently, it is the gluing data of a flat G-bundle over the \
graph, from which a covering (derived) graph can be constructed. The essay \
labels each joint of the base ring with the matrix R_direct or R_twisted \
\[LongDash] a voltage in O(3) \[LongDash] so the 51-fold product around the \
necklace is the loop holonomy; its determinant sign (\[Minus]1)^#twisted is \
the gauge-invariant Z2 parity (the class w_1): even gives the cylinder, odd \
the M\[ODoubleDot]bius band.\
\>", "Text",
 CellMargins->{{104, 30}, {4, 4}},
 FontSize->12,ExpressionUUID->"f5435e5c-cb55-f942-b589-dbe06f3d86ff"],

Cell["\<\
Verify each level live: the cohomology count (all 64 length-6 parity words, \
coboundary flips at every block, exactly two gauge classes, separated by \
holonomy parity); the boundary-strand test on the glued graphs themselves \
(delete the glue rails and count the surviving loops \[LongDash] checked for \
every word of length \[LessEqual] 4, then on the pure-direct, pure-twisted \
and ddt rings); and the exact holonomy determinant of the 51-joint ddt \
necklace:\
\>", "CodeText",ExpressionUUID->"bf0d32ea-8712-ad41-ba95-e630a55cc242"],

Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell["\<\
Module[{flips, coboundaries, orbits, parityInvariantQ, strandCount, \
strandRuleQ, ringStrands, Rd, Rt, unitDet, gapDdt, gapTwisted}, flips = \
Table[ReplacePart[ConstantArray[0, 6], {k -> 1, Mod[k, 6] + 1 -> 1}], {k, \
6}]; coboundaries = DeleteDuplicates[Table[Mod[s . flips, 2], {s, Tuples[{0, \
1}, 6]}]]; orbits = DeleteDuplicates[Table[Sort[(Mod[w + #1, 2] & ) /@ \
coboundaries], {w, Tuples[{0, 1}, 6]}]]; parityInvariantQ = \
Sort[Table[DeleteDuplicates[Mod[Total /@ orb, 2]], {orb, orbits}]] === {{0}, \
{1}}; strandCount[word_String, reps_Integer] := Module[{w, L, u, v, km, \
edges, rails}, w = Characters[StringRepeat[word, reps]]; L = Length[w]; edges \
= Flatten[Table[km = Mod[k - 1, L]; {u, v} = If[w[[km + 1]] === \"d\", {3*km \
+ 1, 3*km + 2}, {3*km + 2, 3*km + 1}]; {{u, v}, {u, 3*k + 1}, {3*k + 1, 3*k + \
2}, {3*k + 2, 3*k + 3}, {3*k + 3, v}}, {k, 0, L - 1}], 1]; rails = Table[{3*k \
+ 1, 3*k + 2}, {k, 0, L - 1}]; Length[ConnectedComponents[Graph[Range[3*L], \
UndirectedEdge @@@ Complement[DeleteDuplicates[Sort /@ edges], rails]]]]]; \
strandRuleQ = AllTrue[Flatten[Table[StringJoin /@ Tuples[{\"d\", \"t\"}, \
len], {len, 1, 4}]], strandCount[#1, 3] == If[EvenQ[3*StringCount[#1, \
\"t\"]], 2, 1] & ]; ringStrands = {\"d\" -> strandCount[\"d\", 17], \"t\" -> \
strandCount[\"t\", 17], \"ddt\" -> strandCount[\"ddt\", 17]}; Rd = \
RotationMatrix[4*(Pi/5), {1, 0, 0}]; Rt = DiagonalMatrix[{1, 1, -1}] . Rd; \
unitDet = FullSimplify[Det[Rt . Rd . Rd]]; gapDdt = 1.40323087 - 4/3; \
gapTwisted = N[Root[49*#1^3 - 128*#1^2 - 75*#1 + 218 & , 2] - 4/3]; \
Association[\"H1(C6;Z2): gauge classes among all 64 length-6 parity words\" \
-> Length[orbits], \"  class invariant is exactly the holonomy parity (-1)^#t\
\" -> parityInvariantQ, \"glue rails deleted: 2 strand loops iff #t even, 1 \
iff odd (all words, len <= 4)\" -> strandRuleQ, \"  this essay's rings: pure \
direct, pure twisted, ddt\" -> ringStrands, \"exact det of the ddt unit cell, \
and of the 51-joint necklace holonomy\" -> {unitDet, unitDet^17}, \"ddt vs \
pure twisted: same w1 class, yet different gap density\" -> \
{OddQ[StringCount[\"ddt\", \"t\"]] == OddQ[StringCount[\"ttt\", \"t\"]], \
{gapDdt, gapTwisted}}]]\
\>", "Input",
 CellOpen->False,ExpressionUUID->"bfc0589b-e44d-b44c-867d-e69294504c09"],

Cell[BoxData[
 RowBox[{"\[LeftAssociation]", 
  RowBox[{
   RowBox[{"\<\"H1(C6;Z2): gauge classes among all 64 length-6 parity words\"\
\>", "\[Rule]", "2"}], ",", 
   RowBox[{"\<\"  class invariant is exactly the holonomy parity (-1)^#t\"\>",
     "\[Rule]", "True"}], ",", 
   RowBox[{"\<\"glue rails deleted: 2 strand loops iff #t even, 1 iff odd \
(all words, len <= 4)\"\>", "\[Rule]", "True"}], ",", 
   RowBox[{"\<\"  this essay's rings: pure direct, pure twisted, ddt\"\>", "\[Rule]", 
    RowBox[{"{", 
     RowBox[{
      RowBox[{"\<\"d\"\>", "\[Rule]", "2"}], ",", 
      RowBox[{"\<\"t\"\>", "\[Rule]", "1"}], ",", 
      RowBox[{"\<\"ddt\"\>", "\[Rule]", "1"}]}], "}"}]}], ",", 
   RowBox[{"\<\"exact det of the ddt unit cell, and of the 51-joint necklace \
holonomy\"\>", "\[Rule]", 
    RowBox[{"{", 
     RowBox[{
      RowBox[{"-", "1"}], ",", 
      RowBox[{"-", "1"}]}], "}"}]}], ",", 
   RowBox[{"\<\"ddt vs pure twisted: same w1 class, yet different gap density\
\"\>", "\[Rule]", 
    RowBox[{"{", 
     RowBox[{"True", ",", 
      RowBox[{"{", 
       RowBox[{"0.06989753666666676`", ",", "0.04338441258252601`"}], "}"}]}],
      "}"}]}]}], "\[RightAssociation]"}]], "Output",ExpressionUUID->"c136cd42-\
767a-cd4e-8963-c3250a349221"]
}, Open  ]],

