Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell["\<\
Why This Resists Proof: De Bruijn Graphs and Ergodic Optimization\
\>", "Section",ExpressionUUID->"98315640-239a-7245-a2ba-16ecc2911be3"],

Cell["\<\
Confirming that ddt really is the best of all infinite gluing words \
\[LongDash] not just the best among the words actually tried \[LongDash] is a \
different kind of problem: an ergodic-optimization (mean-payoff / \
joint-spectral-radius) problem over the shift space of {d,t}-sequences. Every \
length-k window of that shift space is a node of a de Bruijn graph whose \
edges advance the window by one letter; a periodic gluing word is exactly a \
cycle in that graph, and (ddt)\[Infinity] is precisely the 3-cycle ddt \
\[Rule] dtd \[Rule] tdd \[Rule] ddt:\
\>", "Text",ExpressionUUID->"20e8ab68-92a7-3640-9d0c-58da210759c6"],

Cell[TextData[{
 StyleBox["\[FilledRightTriangle] ",
  FontSize->10,
  FontColor->GrayLevel[0.55]],
 StyleBox["ergodic optimization (mean-payoff over shift spaces)",
  FontSize->11,
  FontWeight->"Bold",
  FontColor->GrayLevel[0.3]],
 StyleBox["  \[Bullet] ergodic-optimization problem, mean-payoff problem, \
shift space of {d,t}-sequences, maximizing time-averages over invariant \
measures",
  FontSize->10,
  FontColor->GrayLevel[0.45]]
}], "Text",
 ShowGroupOpener->True,
 CellMargins->{{86, 30}, {1, 
  5}},ExpressionUUID->"e5797a7c-c7b3-6b45-b9cf-d6a4655d4999"],

Cell["\<\
Given a continuous map T: X \[RightArrow] X of a compact metric space and a \
continuous payoff f: X \[RightArrow] \:211d, ergodic optimization studies the \
maximum ergodic average \[Beta](f) = sup \[Integral]f d\[Mu], the supremum \
over all T-invariant Borel probability measures \[Mu]; this supremum is \
attained, and any measure attaining it is a maximizing measure. Equivalently, \
\[Beta](f) is the best achievable long-run time average sup\:2093 lim sup\
\:2099 (1/n)\[CenterDot]\[CapitalSigma]\:2096\:208c\:2080\:207f\:207b\.b9 f(T\
\:1d4fx) \[LongDash] the mean payoff per step. In the essay X is the shift \
space of {d,t}-sequences, T the left shift, and the payoff encodes the gap \
density \[CurlyTheta]/L \[Minus] \[Alpha]/L per pentagon block \[LongDash] a \
whole-word quantity, not letter-local; periodic words correspond to de \
Bruijn-graph cycles, and the optimizer may be a non-periodic (Sturmian) \
sequence \[LongDash] the stated obstruction to certifying (ddt)\[Infinity] as \
globally optimal.\
\>", "Text",
 CellMargins->{{104, 30}, {4, 4}},
 FontSize->12,ExpressionUUID->"ba0c2b88-28e2-2b47-b006-2b8fa8488b4b"],

Cell[TextData[{
 StyleBox["\[FilledRightTriangle] ",
  FontSize->10,
  FontColor->GrayLevel[0.55]],
 StyleBox["de Bruijn graph",
  FontSize->11,
  FontWeight->"Bold",
  FontColor->GrayLevel[0.3]],
 StyleBox["  \[Bullet] de Bruijn-k graph over {d,t}, de Bruijn-3, window \
graph, cycle = periodic word correspondence, de Bruijn machinery",
  FontSize->10,
  FontColor->GrayLevel[0.45]]
}], "Text",
 ShowGroupOpener->True,
 CellMargins->{{86, 30}, {1, 
  5}},ExpressionUUID->"05e85b0f-9bf4-d74b-929d-61b0a30139c7"],

Cell["\<\
The de Bruijn graph of order k over an alphabet \[LongDash] here the gluing \
alphabet {d, t} \[LongDash] has one node for each length-k word and a \
directed edge from w to w\[Prime] exactly when w\[Prime] is obtained from w \
by deleting its first letter and appending one new letter, so following edges \
reads out a sequence one letter at a time. Infinite {d, t}-sequences \
correspond to infinite walks, and periodic gluing words correspond precisely \
to directed cycles: (ddt)\[Infinity] is the 3-cycle ddt \[RightArrow] dtd \
\[RightArrow] tdd \[RightArrow] ddt in the de Bruijn-3 graph. Attaching one \
certificate block to each node is what lets the ladder \[CapitalGamma]_k \
bound the gap density of every gluing word, periodic or not.\
\>", "Text",
 CellMargins->{{104, 30}, {4, 4}},
 FontSize->12,ExpressionUUID->"e10c5bf7-4e9a-214d-b629-4a9ada08107f"],

Cell[TextData[{
 StyleBox["\[FilledRightTriangle] ",
  FontSize->10,
  FontColor->GrayLevel[0.55]],
 StyleBox["joint spectral radius",
  FontSize->11,
  FontWeight->"Bold",
  FontColor->GrayLevel[0.3]],
 StyleBox["  \[Bullet] JSR, joint-spectral-radius problem, maximal growth \
rate of matrix products",
  FontSize->10,
  FontColor->GrayLevel[0.45]]
}], "Text",
 ShowGroupOpener->True,
 CellMargins->{{86, 30}, {1, 
  5}},ExpressionUUID->"0ce376e3-eca8-7f40-8775-48ed2ddb1da6"],

Cell["\<\
The joint spectral radius (JSR) of a finite set of matrices \[CapitalSigma] = \
{A\:2081, \[Ellipsis], A_m} is the maximal exponential growth rate achievable \
by long products drawn from the set: \[Rho](\[CapitalSigma]) = lim_{k\
\[RightArrow]\[Infinity]} max \
\:2016A_{i\:2081}A_{i\:2082}\[CenterEllipsis]A_{i_k}\:2016^(1/k), the maximum \
ranging over all length-k choices of factors (the limit is independent of the \
norm). For a single matrix it reduces to the ordinary spectral radius. \
\[CapitalSigma] has the \"finiteness property\" when some periodic product \
attains \[Rho](\[CapitalSigma]); Bousch and Mairesse showed this can fail, \
the best sequence being aperiodic (Sturmian). This is exactly the obstruction \
the essay imports: certifying (ddt)\[Infinity] as the optimal gluing word \
over {d,t}-sequences is a JSR-type ergodic-optimization problem, so periodic \
search alone cannot close it.\
\>", "Text",
 CellMargins->{{104, 30}, {4, 4}},
 FontSize->12,ExpressionUUID->"971ff80d-498d-c544-a265-9210f9ebebf5"],

Cell["\<\
Build the de Bruijn-3 graph over {d,t}, highlighting the ddt orbit, with the \
direct/twisted rotation-vs-reflection pair recapped below it for reference:\
\>", "CodeText",ExpressionUUID->"d1bdd2e8-7e03-9849-b08e-bacf6bd872c6"],

Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell["\<\
Module[{coral, grayN, grayLine, ink, verts, R, nodeR, labelR, angles, pos, \
labelPos, edgeList, hlEdgeQ, hlNodeQ, trimLine, edgeGr, nodeGr, labelGr, \
deBruijnGraphic, directTwistedSmall}, coral = RGBColor[0.6, 0.24, 0.11]; \
grayN = RGBColor[0.27, 0.27, 0.25]; grayLine = RGBColor[0.78, 0.77, 0.73]; \
ink = GrayLevel[0.15]; verts = {\"ddd\", \"ddt\", \"dtd\", \"tdd\", \"ttt\", \
\"ttd\", \"tdt\", \"dtt\"}; R = 3; nodeR = 0.19; labelR = R + 0.62; angles = \
Table[(90 - 45*k)*Degree, {k, 0, 7}]; pos = AssociationThread[verts, \
Table[R*{Cos[a], Sin[a]}, {a, angles}]]; labelPos = AssociationThread[verts, \
Table[labelR*{Cos[a], Sin[a]}, {a, angles}]]; edgeList = {{\"ddd\", \"ddd\"}, \
{\"ddd\", \"ddt\"}, {\"ddt\", \"dtd\"}, {\"ddt\", \"dtt\"}, {\"dtd\", \
\"tdd\"}, {\"dtd\", \"tdt\"}, {\"dtt\", \"ttd\"}, {\"dtt\", \"ttt\"}, \
{\"tdd\", \"ddd\"}, {\"tdd\", \"ddt\"}, {\"tdt\", \"dtd\"}, {\"tdt\", \
\"dtt\"}, {\"ttd\", \"tdd\"}, {\"ttd\", \"tdt\"}, {\"ttt\", \"ttd\"}, \
{\"ttt\", \"ttt\"}}; hlEdgeQ[{a_, b_}] := MemberQ[{{\"ddt\", \"dtd\"}, {\"dtd\
\", \"tdd\"}, {\"tdd\", \"ddt\"}}, {a, b}]; hlNodeQ[v_] := MemberQ[{\"ddt\", \
\"dtd\", \"tdd\"}, v]; trimLine[a_, b_, gap_:0.26] := Module[{pa = pos[a], pb \
= pos[b], u}, u = (pb - pa)/Norm[pb - pa]; {pa + gap*u, pb - gap*u}]; edgeGr \
= Table[If[e[[1]] == e[[2]], Module[{p = pos[e[[1]]], dir, perp, p1, p2, c1, \
c2}, dir = p/Norm[p]; perp = {-dir[[2]], dir[[1]]}; p1 = p + \
nodeR*Normalize[perp - 0.5*dir]; p2 = p + nodeR*Normalize[perp + 0.5*dir]; c1 \
= p + 1.5*(perp + 0.45*dir); c2 = p + 1.5*(perp - 0.45*dir); {grayLine, \
Thickness[0.0045], Arrowheads[0.05], Arrow[BezierCurve[{p1, c1, c2, p2}]]}], \
Module[{ab = trimLine[e[[1]], e[[2]]]}, {If[hlEdgeQ[e], coral, grayLine], \
Thickness[If[hlEdgeQ[e], 0.0055, 0.0026]], Arrowheads[If[hlEdgeQ[e], 0.03, \
0.02]], Arrow[ab]}]], {e, edgeList}]; nodeGr = Table[{If[hlNodeQ[v], coral, \
grayN], Disk[pos[v], nodeR]}, {v, verts}]; labelGr = Table[Text[Style[v, 16, \
ink], labelPos[v]], {v, verts}]; deBruijnGraphic = Graphics[{edgeGr, nodeGr, \
labelGr}, PlotRange -> 4.35, ImageSize -> 640, ImagePadding -> {{20, 20}, \
{15, 15}}, Background -> White]; directTwistedSmall = Rasterize[Show[fig2[], \
ImagePadding -> {{10, 10}, {10, 10}}], ImageSize -> 380]; \
Column[{deBruijnGraphic, directTwistedSmall}, Spacings -> 0.3, Alignment -> \
Center]]\
\>", "Input",
 CellOpen->False,ExpressionUUID->"4086bf6d-cf5f-4643-8aaa-dfbddd4a6ad5"],

