Cell["\<\
A computer search can only check patterns that repeat \[LongDash] and there \
are uncountably many non-repeating gluing words that no brute force will ever \
reach. The fix is to change what is searched over. The quantity that matters \
\[LongDash] the mesh's advantage per pentagon as it grows without bound \
\[LongDash] is by definition a long-run average, and its precise name is the \
Birkhoff (ergodic) average of a potential \[CurlyPhi] that scores the gap \
contributed at each joint \[LongDash] a potential that depends on the joint's \
surroundings and has no fixed finite range, which is exactly why the windowed \
certificates below approximate it rather than compute it: gap(w) = lim (1/n) \
\[CapitalSigma] \[CurlyPhi](\[Sigma]^j w) over j < n, where \[Sigma] shifts \
the word by one letter. Birkhoff's theorem converts the intractable search \
over individual infinite words into a search over shift-invariant probability \
measures \[LongDash] a compact, convex space that ergodic-optimization theory \
(Ma\[NTilde]\[EAcute], Bousch, Jenkinson) is built to handle. A periodic word \
like (ddt)\[Infinity] is the easiest kind of candidate: it carries a natural \
invariant measure whose average is computable in closed form \[LongDash] the \
gap density used throughout this essay.\
\>", "Text",ExpressionUUID->"e0e2bec2-cd04-f743-84a2-75df75fbfab7"],

Cell["\<\
Certificates built on de Bruijn graphs of increasing window size k give \
successively tighter upper bounds \[CapitalGamma]_k on the best possible gap \
density attainable by any gluing word. Every \[CapitalGamma]_k computed so \
far sits strictly above gap(ddt) (the dashed line) \[LongDash] evidence that \
ddt is close to optimal, and the bound tightens as k grows:\
\>", "Text",ExpressionUUID->"c7e2aa9c-9eef-a34d-ac26-be75692efe6c"],

Cell["\<\
Plot \[CapitalGamma]_7..\[CapitalGamma]_10 against gap(ddt). Each \
\[CapitalGamma]_k comes from a small semidefinite program: one rational \
positive-semidefinite block per de Bruijn-k node, assembled edge-by-edge \
around any ring via a Schur-complement argument into an exact per-block bound \
on \[CurlyTheta]; subtracting the classical term \[Alpha], tracked exactly by \
the same interface dynamic program, and minimizing over all valid block \
choices gives \[CapitalGamma]_k as a provable ceiling on the gap density of \
every gluing word \[LongDash] periodic or not \[LongDash] as seen through \
windows of size k (the semidefinite program itself is not re-solved live here \
\[LongDash] only its already-certified output is plotted; the values are the \
regenerated rational certificates of 2026-07-13, which strictly tighten the \
earlier committed k = 7 and k = 8 bounds):\
\>", "CodeText",ExpressionUUID->"108d07a6-7b08-2c42-b15f-4575c6c42476"],

Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell["\<\
ListLinePlot[{{7, 0.0770206}, {8, 0.0752664}, {9, 0.0720260}, {10, \
0.0714575}}, PlotMarkers -> Automatic, Joined -> True, Epilog -> \
{Directive[Red, Dashed], Line[{{6.5, 0.0698975}, {10.5, 0.0698975}}], \
Text[Style[\"gap(ddt) \[TildeTilde] 0.0699\", 9], {9, 0.0698975}, {0, \
-1.4}]}, AxesLabel -> {\"k\", \"\[CapitalGamma]_k\"}, PlotRange -> {{6.5, \
10.5}, {0.069, 0.078}}, PlotLabel -> \"Certificate ladder \[RightArrow] \
gap(ddt)\", ImageSize -> 360]\
\>", "Input",
 CellOpen->False,ExpressionUUID->"5e2c14ad-7a79-c140-840b-9703a2183e52"],

