Cell["\<\
Why the limit, and not a finite mesh? A finite mesh yields only a partial \
average, and its value drifts with where the reading stops. Only the n \
\[RightArrow] \[Infinity] limit is a property of the pattern itself rather \
than of an arbitrary cutoff \[LongDash] and a larger limit means more quantum \
advantage retained per pentagon, forever, as the mesh grows. That single \
number is exactly what the certificates above bound across all gluing words \
at once, and why gap(ddt) = 0.0699 is the value to beat.\
\>", "Text",ExpressionUUID->"ffdea792-f665-d944-bdc5-b6c5b4f5696e"],

Cell["\<\
But no \[CapitalGamma]_k computed so far equals gap(ddt), and there is no \
guarantee that any finite window ever will: this class of \
ergodic-optimization problems is known to fail the \
\[OpenCurlyDoubleQuote]finiteness property\[CloseCurlyDoubleQuote] in general \
(Bousch\[Dash]Mairesse, 2002) \[LongDash] the true optimum can, in principle, \
be a non-periodic (Sturmian) sequence that no enumeration of periodic words, \
however long, will encounter. Unless the ladder terminates exactly at some \
finite k, extending it can tighten the bracket indefinitely without ever \
closing it.\
\>", "Text",ExpressionUUID->"1602d39e-d123-5f49-92fc-341f4de41777"],

Cell[TextData[{
 StyleBox["\[FilledRightTriangle] ",
  FontSize->10,
  FontColor->GrayLevel[0.55]],
 StyleBox["Sturmian sequence",
  FontSize->11,
  FontWeight->"Bold",
  FontColor->GrayLevel[0.3]],
 StyleBox["  \[Bullet] non-periodic (Sturmian) sequence, Sturmian maximizer, \
aperiodic competitor",
  FontSize->10,
  FontColor->GrayLevel[0.45]]
}], "Text",
 ShowGroupOpener->True,
 CellMargins->{{86, 30}, {1, 
  5}},ExpressionUUID->"d1313256-482a-6344-be2a-baa07c4b49ad"],

Cell["\<\
An infinite sequence over a two-letter alphabet (here d, t) that is aperiodic \
yet has the lowest factor complexity aperiodicity allows: for every n it \
contains exactly n+1 distinct blocks of length n \[LongDash] the minimum \
possible, since by the Morse\[Dash]Hedlund theorem any sequence with at most \
n blocks of some length n is eventually periodic. Equivalently, it is the \
itinerary of an irrational rotation: the n-th letter records whether \:27e8n\
\[Theta] + \[Rho]\:27e9 falls in an interval of length \[Theta] on the \
circle, for irrational \[Theta]. Sturmian sequences are the known shape of \
aperiodic maximizers in ergodic optimization: when the finiteness property \
fails, the best gluing word can be Sturmian, so no search over periodic \
words, however long, will find it.\
\>", "Text",
 CellMargins->{{104, 30}, {4, 4}},
 FontSize->12,ExpressionUUID->"5180e39b-9464-644d-93f0-a2046869cbdc"],

Cell[TextData[{
 StyleBox["\[FilledRightTriangle] ",
  FontSize->10,
  FontColor->GrayLevel[0.55]],
 StyleBox["finiteness property",
  FontSize->11,
  FontWeight->"Bold",
  FontColor->GrayLevel[0.3]],
 StyleBox["  \[Bullet] finiteness conjecture, optimum attained by a periodic \
word / finite matrix product",
  FontSize->10,
  FontColor->GrayLevel[0.45]]
}], "Text",
 ShowGroupOpener->True,
 CellMargins->{{86, 30}, {1, 
  5}},ExpressionUUID->"aa76ce0c-eea6-d648-958f-f4f527de4bbe"],

Cell["\<\
A finite set of matrices {A\:2081, \[Ellipsis], A\:2098} has the finiteness \
property if its joint spectral radius \[Rho] = \
lim_{k\[RightArrow]\[Infinity]} max over length-k words of \:2016A_{i\:2081}\
\[CenterDot]\[CenterDot]\[CenterDot]A_{i_k}\:2016^{1/k} is attained exactly \
by some finite product: \[Rho] = \[Rho](A_{i\:2081}\[CenterDot]\[CenterDot]\
\[CenterDot]A_{i_k})^{1/k} for a word of some finite length k, so that \
repeating that word periodically is globally optimal. The finiteness \
conjecture asserted this always holds; Bousch and Mairesse disproved it, \
exhibiting systems whose optimum is achieved only by non-periodic (Sturmian) \
sequences. Where the property fails \[LongDash] as it can for the essay's \
ergodic optimization over {d, t} gluing words \[LongDash] no enumeration of \
periodic words, however long, can certify a global optimum.\
\>", "Text",
 CellMargins->{{104, 30}, {4, 4}},
 FontSize->12,ExpressionUUID->"d6d5dee9-6d9a-9148-8a44-afed8c88f286"]
}, Open  ]],

Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell["From Gluing Words to Black-Box Certification", "Section",ExpressionUUID->"86d17985-334c-e241-986e-7be9a42b91d4"],

Cell["\<\
Everything so far is composition mathematics; this closing section is what it \
is for \[LongDash] and why the essay carries the title it does. Return to the \
sealed box that opened the first section: a device claims to be quantum, and \
only its click statistics can be observed. The pentagon is the smallest \
interrogation protocol whose quantum answer no classical hidden-variable \
account can match, and the gap \[CurlyTheta] - \[Alpha] is the margin of that \
impossibility \[LongDash] the certifiable advantage per interrogation round. \
A mesh is therefore a certification instrument, and its gluing word is a \
design parameter: it sets how the margin scales with instrument size. Direct \
rings collapse the margin outright (\[CurlyTheta] = \[Alpha] at even N: a \
larger instrument with zero statistical power); twisted rings hold a fixed \
margin of \[Tau]* - 4/3 \[TildeTilde] 0.0434 per pentagon; the ddt word \
raises it to \[TildeTilde] 0.0699 \[LongDash] the 61% of the abstract. \
Optimizing gap density, the business of the preceding sections, is optimizing \
the statistical power of a black-box test per unit of hardware.\
\>", "Text",ExpressionUUID->"56fe4d3a-9966-cf46-b774-bef228448ff0"],

Cell[TextData[{
 StyleBox["\[FilledRightTriangle] ",
  FontSize->10,
  FontColor->GrayLevel[0.55]],
 StyleBox["Black-box / device-independent certification",
  FontSize->11,
  FontWeight->"Bold",
  FontColor->GrayLevel[0.3]],
 StyleBox["  \[Bullet] black-box certification instrument, certification \
instrument, black-box test, certifiable advantage per interrogation round, \
sealed box, black-box physics",
  FontSize->10,
  FontColor->GrayLevel[0.45]]
}], "Text",
 ShowGroupOpener->True,
 CellMargins->{{86, 30}, {1, 
  5}},ExpressionUUID->"1eb57c68-29b0-aa42-a8f6-5d3af6189167"],

Cell["\<\
Certifying a quantum property of a device from its observed input-output \
statistics alone, treating its interior as a sealed box whose workings are \
neither trusted nor modelled. The certificate is a correlation that exceeds a \
bound provably obeyed by every noncontextual classical account: here the \
noncontextual limit \[Alpha], which the quantum value \[CurlyTheta] beats, so \
a click table with \[CapitalSigma]p above \[Alpha] could not have come from \
any noncontextual hidden-variable device. The gap \[CurlyTheta] \[Minus] \
\[Alpha] is the certifiable advantage per interrogation round; self-testing, \
inferring the state and measurements themselves, is its strongest form. \
Statistics alone cannot, however, unmask a classical intensity forger \
\[LongDash] excluding that impostor is what the essay's dynamics audit adds.\
\>", "Text",
 CellMargins->{{104, 30}, {4, 4}},
 FontSize->12,ExpressionUUID->"8a407e68-9543-634d-8ffa-3ef6cc96c5b6"],

Cell[TextData[{
 StyleBox["\[FilledRightTriangle] ",
  FontSize->10,
  FontColor->GrayLevel[0.55]],
 StyleBox["Statistical power / test statistic",
  FontSize->11,
  FontWeight->"Bold",
  FontColor->GrayLevel[0.3]],
 StyleBox["  \[Bullet] statistical power, statistical power per pentagon, \
statistical power of a black-box test per unit of hardware, margin of \
impossibility, certifiable advantage per round",
  FontSize->10,
  FontColor->GrayLevel[0.45]]
}], "Text",
 ShowGroupOpener->True,
 CellMargins->{{86, 30}, {1, 
  5}},ExpressionUUID->"5b950add-d47c-2741-924b-9a5af2252185"],

Cell["\<\
In hypothesis testing, a test statistic is the single number computed from \
the data on which the accept/reject decision rests, and the statistical power \
of a test is the probability it correctly rejects the null hypothesis when a \
specified alternative holds \[LongDash] 1 minus the Type II (false-negative) \
error rate. Here the test statistic is the observed event-sum \
\[CapitalSigma]p; the null hypothesis is the classical noncontextual \
(hidden-variable) account, capped at \[Alpha], and the alternative is the \
genuinely quantum device reaching \[CurlyTheta]. The margin \[CurlyTheta] \
\[Minus] \[Alpha] is what lets finite click data reject the classical \
account; dividing by chain length L, the gap density \[CurlyTheta]/L \[Minus] \
\[Alpha]/L measures the certifying power each glued pentagon contributes, so \
optimizing gap density optimizes statistical power per unit of hardware.\
\>", "Text",
 CellMargins->{{104, 30}, {4, 4}},
 FontSize->12,ExpressionUUID->"49fb7096-649d-3d46-89c9-07c3ef959aeb"],

Cell["\<\
The quantum side of such a test stays remarkably cheap. The twisted-chain \
bound is realized, at every length, by the same three-level system Lapkiewicz \
et al. (2011) drove through one pentagon \[LongDash] a single photon on three \
optical paths; growing the mesh grows only the list of measurement settings, \
never the dimension. The noise requirement does not run away with size \
either: under isotropic noise the critical visibility \[LongDash] the signal \
fraction below which the quantum advantage disappears \[LongDash] rises from \
0.5854 for a single pentagon but saturates at 1/(3(\[Tau]* - 1)) \
\[TildeTilde] 0.8848 in the long-chain limit. A fixed ~88.5% visibility \
target, independent of instrument size, is an unusually forgiving scaling law \
for an entanglement-free contextuality experiment.\
\>", "Text",ExpressionUUID->"06d88396-4a5a-0d44-9ce1-17157603dc5a"],

Cell[TextData[{
 StyleBox["\[FilledRightTriangle] ",
  FontSize->10,
  FontColor->GrayLevel[0.55]],
 StyleBox["Critical visibility",
  FontSize->11,
  FontWeight->"Bold",
  FontColor->GrayLevel[0.3]],
 StyleBox["  \[Bullet] critical visibility, the signal fraction below which \
the quantum advantage disappears, ~88.5% visibility target, 0.5854, \
1/(3(tau*-1)) approx 0.8848",
  FontSize->10,
  FontColor->GrayLevel[0.45]]
}], "Text",
 ShowGroupOpener->True,
 CellMargins->{{86, 30}, {1, 
  5}},ExpressionUUID->"e032b28b-3c6c-8148-810f-6958a6311628"],

Cell["\<\
Critical visibility is the threshold mixing fraction v at which a \
contextuality test loses its quantum advantage under isotropic noise. \
Modeling the noisy qutrit as \[Rho]_v = v\[CenterDot]\[Rho] + (1\[Minus]v)\
\[CenterDot]I/3, the event-sum witness \[CapitalSigma]p interpolates between \
its ideal quantum value and the fully mixed value of 1/3 per event; v_crit is \
the v at which \[CapitalSigma]p drops to the classical noncontextual bound \
\[Alpha], so for v \[LessEqual] v_crit a noncontextual model exists and the \
advantage disappears. A single pentagon gives v_crit = (2 \[Minus] 5/3)/(\
\[Sqrt]5 \[Minus] 5/3) \[TildeTilde] 0.5854, rising to \
1/(3(\[Tau]*\[Minus]1)) \[TildeTilde] 0.8848 in the long-chain limit.\
\>", "Text",
 CellMargins->{{104, 30}, {4, 4}},
 FontSize->12,ExpressionUUID->"147aa813-720e-7b48-b224-b74002da2311"],

Cell[TextData[{
 StyleBox["\[FilledRightTriangle] ",
  FontSize->10,
  FontColor->GrayLevel[0.55]],
 StyleBox["Isotropic (white / depolarizing) noise",
  FontSize->11,
  FontWeight->"Bold",
  FontColor->GrayLevel[0.3]],
 StyleBox["  \[Bullet] isotropic noise, white noise, depolarizing noise",
  FontSize->10,
  FontColor->GrayLevel[0.45]]
}], "Text",
 ShowGroupOpener->True,
 CellMargins->{{86, 30}, {1, 
  5}},ExpressionUUID->"4c274f08-77ae-e645-9ca5-4afe18f570d2"],

Cell["\<\
A one-parameter noise model in which an ideal d-dimensional state \[Rho] is \
blended with the maximally mixed (identity) state I/d: \[Rho]_v = v\
\[CenterDot]\[Rho] + (1\[Minus]v)\[CenterDot]I/d. This is the action of the \
depolarizing channel, which with probability 1\[Minus]v discards the input \
and replaces it by the featureless I/d, leaving the direction of \[Rho] \
untouched while shrinking every deviation from I/d uniformly. The visibility \
v \[Element] [0,1] is the surviving signal fraction. The critical visibility \
is the smallest v at which the state still beats the noncontextual bound; \
below it, isotropic mixing has washed the contextual advantage away.\
\>", "Text",
 CellMargins->{{104, 30}, {4, 4}},
 FontSize->12,ExpressionUUID->"df4e63c2-7eb6-c149-a210-7d0b90df8ba8"],

Cell["\<\
Both endpoints of that visibility law, exactly \[LongDash] the \
single-pentagon value and the long-chain ceiling, from the same algebraic \
\[Tau]*:\
\>", "CodeText",ExpressionUUID->"a979726e-8644-1d42-97bf-e4e902b3192b"],

Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell["\<\
tauStar = Root[49 #^3 - 128 #^2 - 75 # + 218 &, 2];
{FullSimplify[(2 - 5/3)/(Sqrt[5] - 5/3)], N[(2 - 5/3)/(Sqrt[5] - 5/3), 5],
 N[1/(3 (tauStar - 1)), 5]}\
\>", "Input",
 CellOpen->False,ExpressionUUID->"80b70d6e-843b-8446-b5ee-a013cd329db5"],

Cell[BoxData[
 RowBox[{"{", 
  RowBox[{
   RowBox[{
    FractionBox["1", "20"], " ", 
    RowBox[{"(", 
     RowBox[{"5", "+", 
      RowBox[{"3", " ", 
       SqrtBox["5"]}]}], ")"}]}], ",", "0.5854101966249684545`5.", ",", 
   "0.8848357608504698903`5."}], "}"}]], "Output",ExpressionUUID->"6642ddc2-\
b180-464d-b03c-6e73d828058e"]
}, Open  ]],

Cell["\<\
Now the title's converse question: could a classical optical device fake the \
box? On statistics alone the uncomfortable answer is yes, in principle: a \
classical light beam, split and redistributed with tuned intensities, can \
reproduce any consistent table of click frequencies \[LongDash] the \
quantum-optimal one included. That is a theorem, not a defect of imagination, \
and it has a sharp corollary: no function of the observed table alone can \
bound what the device's internal dynamics generate. Breaking the tie takes a \
second, independent lens \[LongDash] audit the dynamics a claimed optical \
design is even capable of generating. Passive linear optics, the \
beamsplitters and phase shifters an intensity forger is limited to, close \
inside the compact algebra u(n), of dimension at most n^2 on n modes; \
squeezing escapes into the full symplectic algebra sp(2n,R), of dimension \
n(2n+1). Which side a design falls on is decidable by an exact-arithmetic \
rank computation, polynomial in the number of modes \[LongDash] in sharp \
contrast to the qubit-side Lie closure, which grows as 4^n - 1 and stalls at \
two glued pentagons.\
\>", "Text",ExpressionUUID->"aad4cbcb-4438-0943-b90f-b1804e0c8f85"],

Cell[TextData[{
 StyleBox["\[FilledRightTriangle] ",
  FontSize->10,
  FontColor->GrayLevel[0.55]],
 StyleBox["Passive linear optics (beamsplitters and phase shifters)",
  FontSize->11,
  FontWeight->"Bold",
  FontColor->GrayLevel[0.3]],
 StyleBox["  \[Bullet] passive linear optics, beamsplitters and phase \
shifters, passive two-mode interferometer, passive interferometer, intensity \
forger's toolkit, phase[n,j], bsRe/bsIm",
  FontSize->10,
  FontColor->GrayLevel[0.45]]
}], "Text",
 ShowGroupOpener->True,
 CellMargins->{{86, 30}, {1, 
  5}},ExpressionUUID->"636f4c3a-0cb7-2643-9047-7d6dfadbf3c0"],

Cell["\<\
Passive linear optics is the class of optical transformations built solely \
from beamsplitters and phase shifters \[LongDash] devices that redistribute \
and rephase light among n modes without amplifying it, conserving total \
photon number. In the essay's quadrature representation each declared \
generator (phase[n,j], bsRe, bsIm) is real antisymmetric, and iterated \
commutators of such generators close inside the compact unitary algebra u(n), \
the n\[Times]n anti-Hermitian matrices of dimension at most n\.b2. Reck, \
Zeilinger, Bernstein and Bertani showed every n-mode unitary factors into \
beamsplitters and phase shifters, so u(n) is exactly an intensity forger's \
reach; escaping it into the symplectic algebra sp(2n,R) requires squeezing.\
\>", "Text",
 CellMargins->{{104, 30}, {4, 4}},
 FontSize->12,ExpressionUUID->"7905b6a4-f991-df4d-8d2e-2a79f901bd3d"],

Cell[TextData[{
 StyleBox["\[FilledRightTriangle] ",
  FontSize->10,
  FontColor->GrayLevel[0.55]],
 StyleBox["Squeezing / single- and two-mode squeezers",
  FontSize->11,
  FontWeight->"Bold",
  FontColor->GrayLevel[0.3]],
 StyleBox["  \[Bullet] squeezing, single squeezed mode, single-mode squeezer \
(sq1), two-mode squeezer (sq2Re/sq2Im), pair production at the horizon as a \
two-mode squeezer, active optics",
  FontSize->10,
  FontColor->GrayLevel[0.45]]
}], "Text",
 ShowGroupOpener->True,
 CellMargins->{{86, 30}, {1, 
  5}},ExpressionUUID->"d9f6e3f9-5495-7a43-ae05-bb33170032ef"],

Cell["\<\
Squeezing is the optical operation generated by Hamiltonians quadratic in the \
mode creation and annihilation operators \[LongDash] a single-mode squeezer \
(sq1) by terms in a\.b2 and a\[Dagger]\.b2, a two-mode squeezer (sq2Re/sq2Im) \
by a\[Dagger]b\[Dagger] and ab \[LongDash] which redistribute quadrature \
noise, pushing one quadrature's variance below the vacuum level at its \
conjugate's expense. Unlike passive phase shifters and beamsplitters, which \
conserve total photon number and close inside the compact algebra u(n), these \
number-nonconserving generators escape into the full symplectic algebra \
sp(2n,R). A design requiring them is active, not passive, optics; in the \
analogue-Hawking channel, pair production at the horizon acts as a two-mode \
squeezer.\
\>", "Text",
 CellMargins->{{104, 30}, {4, 4}},
 FontSize->12,ExpressionUUID->"fd9730a8-84be-954b-b87e-50c989d0a148"],

Cell[TextData[{
 StyleBox["\[FilledRightTriangle] ",
  FontSize->10,
  FontColor->GrayLevel[0.55]],
 StyleBox["Unitary Lie algebra u(n) / unitary group (compact algebra)",
  FontSize->11,
  FontWeight->"Bold",
  FontColor->GrayLevel[0.3]],
 StyleBox["  \[Bullet] compact algebra u(n), u(n), u(2), dimension at most \
n^2, compact algebra",
  FontSize->10,
  FontColor->GrayLevel[0.45]]
}], "Text",
 ShowGroupOpener->True,
 CellMargins->{{86, 30}, {1, 
  5}},ExpressionUUID->"492ab914-9f21-3141-92f0-ec5507be6b2e"],

Cell["\<\
The unitary group U(n) is the group of n\[Times]n complex matrices U \
satisfying U\[Dagger]U = I (columns orthonormal); it is compact, and its Lie \
algebra u(n) consists of the n\[Times]n anti-Hermitian matrices (X\[Dagger] = \
\[Minus]X), a real vector space of dimension n\.b2. These are the generators \
of energy- and photon-number-conserving evolutions \[LongDash] passive linear \
optics (beamsplitters and phase shifters) on n modes. The essay's audit \
closes declared generators under commutators in the quadrature \
representation; if every element stays antisymmetric and the rank stays \
\[LessEqual] n\.b2 \[LongDash] i.e. inside u(n) \[LongDash] the design is \
passive-emulable, while squeezing generators grow the closure out to the \
larger symplectic algebra sp(2n,R), of dimension n(2n+1).\
\>", "Text",
 CellMargins->{{104, 30}, {4, 4}},
 FontSize->12,ExpressionUUID->"162d8268-d666-c94e-bda6-5bf7eb96991b"],

Cell[TextData[{
 StyleBox["\[FilledRightTriangle] ",
  FontSize->10,
  FontColor->GrayLevel[0.55]],
 StyleBox["Real symplectic Lie algebra sp(2n,R) / symplectic group",
  FontSize->11,
  FontWeight->"Bold",
  FontColor->GrayLevel[0.3]],
 StyleBox["  \[Bullet] symplectic algebra sp(2n,R), sp(2n,R), sp(4,R), \
sp(2,R), full symplectic algebra, dimension n(2n+1)",
  FontSize->10,
  FontColor->GrayLevel[0.45]]
}], "Text",
 ShowGroupOpener->True,
 CellMargins->{{86, 30}, {1, 
  5}},ExpressionUUID->"6bab7b53-2470-124e-8044-2714af872fd2"],

Cell["\<\
The real symplectic group Sp(2n,R) is the group of real 2n\[Times]2n matrices \
M preserving the symplectic form \[CapitalOmega], M\:1d40\[CapitalOmega]M = \
\[CapitalOmega]; on n bosonic modes it generates the linear (Gaussian) \
quadrature dynamics. Its Lie algebra sp(2n,R) consists of the real \
2n\[Times]2n matrices A satisfying A\:1d40\[CapitalOmega] + \[CapitalOmega]A \
= 0, and has dimension n(2n+1). It is noncompact, and contains the compact \
u(n) of passive beamsplitter-and-phase optics as a subalgebra (dimension \
n\.b2); the extra generators are squeezers. In the audit here, a passive \
two-mode interferometer stays inside u(2) (dimension 4), adding the two-mode \
squeezer closes all of sp(4,R) (dimension 10), and a single squeezed mode \
already fills sp(2,R) (dimension 3).\
\>", "Text",
 CellMargins->{{104, 30}, {4, 4}},
 FontSize->12,ExpressionUUID->"d9856922-bec9-564c-95be-8a982dde53bb"],

Cell[TextData[{
 StyleBox["\[FilledRightTriangle] ",
  FontSize->10,
  FontColor->GrayLevel[0.55]],
 StyleBox["Dynamical Lie algebra (DLA) / iterated-commutator (Lie) closure",
  FontSize->11,
  FontWeight->"Bold",
  FontColor->GrayLevel[0.3]],
 StyleBox["  \[Bullet] Lie closure, iterated-commutator closure, closure, \
closure dimension, dynamical Lie algebra, qubit-side Lie closure grows as 4^n \
- 1",
  FontSize->10,
  FontColor->GrayLevel[0.45]]
}], "Text",
 ShowGroupOpener->True,
 CellMargins->{{86, 30}, {1, 
  5}},ExpressionUUID->"70fd3528-b223-164f-90d2-5a6d146ee790"],

Cell["\<\
The dynamical Lie algebra of a set of generators \[LongDash] here the \
declared optical generators (phases, beamsplitters, squeezers), written in \
the 2n\[Times]2n real quadrature representation \[LongDash] is the smallest \
Lie algebra containing them and closed under commutation [A,B] = AB \[Minus] \
BA. The audit computes it by repeatedly appending commutators until the \
spanned dimension stops growing (its FixedPoint closure), reading off that \
dimension by exact rank. It then asks whether the closure stays inside the \
compact algebra u(n) \[LongDash] realized here as antisymmetric matrices, \
dimension at most n\.b2 \[LongDash] or reaches the full real symplectic \
algebra sp(2n,R), the matrices M with M\:1d40\[CapitalOmega] + \
\[CapitalOmega]M = 0 for the symplectic form \[CapitalOmega], dimension \
n(2n+1). The DLA fixes which dynamics the generators can reach; the passive \
verdict requires every closure element antisymmetric and closure dimension at \
most n\.b2.\
\>", "Text",
 CellMargins->{{104, 30}, {4, 4}},
 FontSize->12,ExpressionUUID->"3be50e60-0581-8b42-9b2d-d347e751dfcd"],

Cell[TextData[{
 StyleBox["\[FilledRightTriangle] ",
  FontSize->10,
  FontColor->GrayLevel[0.55]],
 StyleBox["Antisymmetric-matrix (compact-algebra) characterization",
  FontSize->11,
  FontWeight->"Bold",
  FontColor->GrayLevel[0.3]],
 StyleBox["  \[Bullet] every element antisymmetric, Transpose[#] === -#, \
skew-symmetric generators, compact-algebra characterization, staying inside \
u(n) means antisymmetric",
  FontSize->10,
  FontColor->GrayLevel[0.45]]
}], "Text",
 ShowGroupOpener->True,
 CellMargins->{{86, 30}, {1, 
  5}},ExpressionUUID->"7b53ae4f-d9b0-b247-9d44-3a90ee672677"],

Cell["\<\
In the real quadrature representation on n optical modes, the audit writes \
each declared generator as a 2n\[Times]2n matrix acting on \
position\[Dash]momentum coordinates; every such matrix lies in the symplectic \
algebra sp(2n,R) = {M : M\:1d40\[CapitalOmega] + \[CapitalOmega]M = 0} for \
the symplectic form \[CapitalOmega], dimension n(2n+1). The compact-algebra \
characterization pins down its maximal compact subalgebra u(n) \[LongDash] \
the passive beamsplitter-and-phase generators, dimension n\.b2 \[LongDash] \
via the criterion that a symplectic generator lies in u(n) exactly when its \
quadrature matrix is antisymmetric, Transpose[M] === \[Minus]M. The verdict \
tests the full commutator closure: every element antisymmetric and closure \
rank at most n\.b2 means the design is passive-linear-optics reproducible; \
any element escaping antisymmetry signals genuine squeezing into the full \
sp(2n,R).\
\>", "Text",
 CellMargins->{{104, 30}, {4, 4}},
 FontSize->12,ExpressionUUID->"37ae0198-2e8a-c54d-9f40-c9651d830996"],

Cell[TextData[{
 StyleBox["\[FilledRightTriangle] ",
  FontSize->10,
  FontColor->GrayLevel[0.55]],
 StyleBox["Classical intensity emulator (intensity forger)",
  FontSize->11,
  FontWeight->"Bold",
  FontColor->GrayLevel[0.3]],
 StyleBox["  \[Bullet] classical intensity emulators, intensity forger, \
classical light beam split and redistributed with tuned intensities, \
classical fake, impostor",
  FontSize->10,
  FontColor->GrayLevel[0.45]]
}], "Text",
 ShowGroupOpener->True,
 CellMargins->{{86, 30}, {1, 
  5}},ExpressionUUID->"038a80a9-4630-1041-8fb4-b0073cb95903"],

Cell["\<\
A classical intensity emulator (intensity forger) is an adversarial device \
that imitates a quantum black box using only a classical light beam split and \
redistributed across modes with tuned intensities. Because such a beam can \
reproduce any consistent table of click frequencies \[LongDash] the \
quantum-optimal one included \[LongDash] statistics alone cannot certify a \
genuinely quantum interior, which is why the essay adds a dynamics audit. The \
forger is confined to passive linear optics (phase shifters and \
beamsplitters), whose generators close inside the compact algebra u(n) of n\
\[Times]n anti-Hermitian matrices, dimension n\.b2; genuine squeezing would \
instead escape into sp(2n,R), dimension n(2n+1). Against exactly this \
impostor class the certification battery is provably complete \[LongDash] a \
conditional theorem resting on one stated detector-physics premise.\
\>", "Text",
 CellMargins->{{104, 30}, {4, 4}},
 FontSize->12,ExpressionUUID->"6378746b-937b-dd47-b0c9-6bee9b32da25"],

Cell["\<\
The audit, self-contained \[LongDash] exact iterated-commutator closure of \
the declared generators in the quadrature representation; staying inside u(n) \
(every element antisymmetric, dimension \[LessEqual] n^2) means the design is \
reproducible by passive linear optics:\
\>", "CodeText",ExpressionUUID->"cddde383-3b86-d541-a966-779c35f7bd0e"],

Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell["\<\
comm[a_, b_] := a . b - b . a;
rk[ms_] := If[ms === {}, 0, MatrixRank[Flatten /@ ms]];
indep[ms_] := Fold[If[rk[Append[#1, #2]] > rk[#1], Append[#1, #2], #1] &, {}, \
ms];
closure[gens_] :=
  FixedPoint[indep[Join[#, Flatten[Outer[comm, #, #, 1], 1]]] &, indep[gens]];
omega[n_] := KroneckerProduct[IdentityMatrix[n], {{0, 1}, {-1, 0}}];
qf[n_, terms_] := Total[Module[{m = ConstantArray[0, {2 n, 2 n}]},
     m[[#1, #2]] += #3/2; m[[#2, #1]] += #3/2; m] & @@@ terms];
gen[n_, terms_] := omega[n] . qf[n, terms];
phase[n_, j_] := gen[n, {{2 j - 1, 2 j - 1, 1}, {2 j, 2 j, 1}}];
bsRe[n_, j_, k_] := gen[n, {{2 j - 1, 2 k - 1, 1}, {2 j, 2 k, 1}}];
bsIm[n_, j_, k_] := gen[n, {{2 j - 1, 2 k, 1}, {2 j, 2 k - 1, -1}}];
sq1[n_, j_] := gen[n, {{2 j - 1, 2 j - 1, 1}, {2 j, 2 j, -1}}];
sq2Re[n_, j_, k_] := gen[n, {{2 j - 1, 2 k - 1, 1}, {2 j, 2 k, -1}}];
sq2Im[n_, j_, k_] := gen[n, {{2 j - 1, 2 k, 1}, {2 j, 2 k - 1, 1}}];
audit[gens_, n_] := Module[{b = closure[gens]},
   <|\"closure dimension\" -> rk[b], \"u(n) dimension\" -> n^2,
    \"sp(2n,R) dimension\" -> n (2 n + 1),
    \"verdict\" -> If[AllTrue[b, Transpose[#] === -# &] && rk[b] <= n^2,
      \"passive-confined: emulable by linear optics\",
      \"active: genuine squeezing, beyond passive optics\"]|>];
Column[{\"passive interferometer, 2 modes (phases + beamsplitter):\",
  audit[{phase[2, 1], phase[2, 2], bsRe[2, 1, 2], bsIm[2, 1, 2]}, 2],
  \"analogue-Hawking channel (phases + graybody beamsplitter + two-mode \
squeezer):\",
  audit[{phase[2, 1], phase[2, 2], bsRe[2, 1, 2], bsIm[2, 1, 2],
    sq2Re[2, 1, 2], sq2Im[2, 1, 2]}, 2],
  \"single squeezed mode (squeezer + phase), 1 mode:\",
  audit[{phase[1, 1], sq1[1, 1]}, 1]}, Spacings -> 0.8]\
\>", "Input",
 CellOpen->False,ExpressionUUID->"2c910698-002c-4b42-ba0e-54971eafa857"],

Cell[BoxData[
 TagBox[GridBox[{
    {"\<\"passive interferometer, 2 modes (phases + beamsplitter):\"\>"},
    {
     RowBox[{"\[LeftAssociation]", 
      RowBox[{
       RowBox[{"\<\"closure dimension\"\>", "\[Rule]", "4"}], ",", 
       RowBox[{"\<\"u(n) dimension\"\>", "\[Rule]", "4"}], ",", 
       RowBox[{"\<\"sp(2n,R) dimension\"\>", "\[Rule]", "10"}], ",", 
       RowBox[{"\<\"verdict\"\>", 
        "\[Rule]", "\<\"passive-confined: emulable by linear optics\"\>"}]}], 
      "\[RightAssociation]"}]},
    {"\<\"analogue-Hawking channel (phases + graybody beamsplitter + two-mode \
squeezer):\"\>"},
    {
     RowBox[{"\[LeftAssociation]", 
      RowBox[{
       RowBox[{"\<\"closure dimension\"\>", "\[Rule]", "10"}], ",", 
       RowBox[{"\<\"u(n) dimension\"\>", "\[Rule]", "4"}], ",", 
       RowBox[{"\<\"sp(2n,R) dimension\"\>", "\[Rule]", "10"}], ",", 
       RowBox[{"\<\"verdict\"\>", 
        "\[Rule]", "\<\"active: genuine squeezing, beyond passive \
optics\"\>"}]}], "\[RightAssociation]"}]},
    {"\<\"single squeezed mode (squeezer + phase), 1 mode:\"\>"},
    {
     RowBox[{"\[LeftAssociation]", 
      RowBox[{
       RowBox[{"\<\"closure dimension\"\>", "\[Rule]", "3"}], ",", 
       RowBox[{"\<\"u(n) dimension\"\>", "\[Rule]", "1"}], ",", 
       RowBox[{"\<\"sp(2n,R) dimension\"\>", "\[Rule]", "3"}], ",", 
       RowBox[{"\<\"verdict\"\>", 
        "\[Rule]", "\<\"active: genuine squeezing, beyond passive \
optics\"\>"}]}], "\[RightAssociation]"}]}
   },
   DefaultBaseStyle->"Column",
   GridBoxAlignment->{"Columns" -> {{Left}}},
   GridBoxItemSize->{"Columns" -> {{Automatic}}, "Rows" -> {{Automatic}}},
   GridBoxSpacings->{"Columns" -> {{Automatic}}, "Rows" -> {{0.8}}}],
  "Column"]], "Output",ExpressionUUID->"59da147f-1551-344b-b40e-1135d3e27813"]
}, Open  ]],

Cell[TextData[{
 StyleBox["\[FilledRightTriangle] ",
  FontSize->10,
  FontColor->GrayLevel[0.55]],
 StyleBox["Quadrature representation / quadrature operators",
  FontSize->11,
  FontWeight->"Bold",
  FontColor->GrayLevel[0.3]],
 StyleBox["  \[Bullet] quadrature representation, quadratures, symplectic \
form omega[n], the 2n x 2n real generator matrices, quadratic-form generators \
qf",
  FontSize->10,
  FontColor->GrayLevel[0.45]]
}], "Text",
 ShowGroupOpener->True,
 CellMargins->{{86, 30}, {1, 
  5}},ExpressionUUID->"3a6e5299-0aea-194f-893f-ca8c1998463c"],

Cell["\<\
In the continuous-variable picture used here, each optical mode j carries two \
Hermitian quadrature operators \[LongDash] a position-like x\:0302_j and a \
momentum-like p\:0302_j, obeying the canonical commutator [x\:0302_j, \
p\:0302_k] = i \[Delta]_jk. The 2n quadratures of n modes stack into one real \
vector whose commutation structure is packaged by the symplectic form \
\[CapitalOmega] (the code's omega[n], n block-diagonal copies of \
[[0,1],[-1,0]]). Any Hamiltonian quadratic in these quadratures generates a \
linear, \[CapitalOmega]-preserving flow, so its generator is a real \
2n\[Times]2n matrix \[CapitalOmega]\[CenterDot]qf acting on the quadrature \
vector \[LongDash] which is why the audit's generators are real 2n\[Times]2n \
matrices, passive optics closing in u(n) and squeezing reaching sp(2n,R).\
\>", "Text",
 CellMargins->{{104, 30}, {4, 4}},
 FontSize->12,ExpressionUUID->"5528f875-b801-8140-bacc-8f2d29e885e4"],

Cell[TextData[{
 StyleBox["\[FilledRightTriangle] ",
  FontSize->10,
  FontColor->GrayLevel[0.55]],
 StyleBox["Analogue Hawking radiation / analogue gravity",
  FontSize->11,
  FontWeight->"Bold",
  FontColor->GrayLevel[0.3]],
 StyleBox["  \[Bullet] analogue-Hawking channel, analogue-Hawking dynamics, \
horizon, pair production at the horizon, two-mode squeezer that models a \
horizon, free phase evolution + graybody backscatter + pair production",
  FontSize->10,
  FontColor->GrayLevel[0.45]]
}], "Text",
 ShowGroupOpener->True,
 CellMargins->{{86, 30}, {1, 
  5}},ExpressionUUID->"a0cd4ae4-aea2-c54d-b165-5704efe8c264"],

Cell["\<\
Analogue gravity is a research programme that reproduces features of \
curved-spacetime physics \[LongDash] event horizons above all \[LongDash] \
inside laboratory systems such as flowing fluids or nonlinear optical media, \
where small perturbations propagate along an effective metric instead of in \
real gravity. Analogue Hawking radiation is the thermal pair-creation such a \
mimicked horizon emits, the tabletop counterpart of a black hole's Hawking \
radiation. In this essay's audit the analogue-Hawking channel is modeled on \
two optical modes as free phase evolution, a graybody-backscatter \
beamsplitter, and horizon pair production as a two-mode squeezer; that \
squeezer lifts the dynamical Lie algebra from u(2) to the full sp(4,R), \
producing the \"beyond passive optics\" verdict.\
\>", "Text",
 CellMargins->{{104, 30}, {4, 4}},
 FontSize->12,ExpressionUUID->"eaa40e4b-39f0-3348-9fc8-5a8b03bcbad6"],

Cell[TextData[{
 StyleBox["\[FilledRightTriangle] ",
  FontSize->10,
  FontColor->GrayLevel[0.55]],
 StyleBox["Graybody factor",
  FontSize->11,
  FontWeight->"Bold",
  FontColor->GrayLevel[0.3]],
 StyleBox["  \[Bullet] graybody beamsplitter, graybody backscatter, greybody \
factor",
  FontSize->10,
  FontColor->GrayLevel[0.45]]
}], "Text",
 ShowGroupOpener->True,
 CellMargins->{{86, 30}, {1, 
  5}},ExpressionUUID->"4779f3c6-6a35-2048-a341-558160b2a6c0"],

Cell["\<\
In black-hole physics, Hawking emission is not a perfect blackbody: quanta \
escaping the horizon backscatter off the surrounding curvature potential \
barrier, and the graybody factor \[CapitalGamma](\[Omega]) is the \
frequency-dependent transmission probability, 0 \[LessEqual] \[CapitalGamma](\
\[Omega]) \[LessEqual] 1, that multiplies the ideal Planck spectrum to give \
the actual emitted flux \[LongDash] the measure of how far the spectrum \
departs from a blackbody. In the essay's analogue-Hawking audit this horizon \
transmission is represented as a passive beamsplitter \[LongDash] the \
graybody beamsplitter generator (bsRe/bsIm) \[LongDash] mixing the two \
optical modes between free phase evolution and the two-mode squeezer that \
models pair production.\
\>", "Text",
 CellMargins->{{104, 30}, {4, 4}},
 FontSize->12,ExpressionUUID->"be6946c7-77b9-e948-bc0f-6d8ff3b43fc2"],

Cell["\<\
Run on three declared designs, the audit returns computed verdicts, not \
promises: a passive two-mode interferometer stays confined at dimension 4 = \
u(2) \[LongDash] emulable; add the two-mode squeezer that models a horizon \
and the closure jumps to dimension 10, all of sp(4,R) \[LongDash] active, \
beyond any passive-optics emulation; even a single squeezed mode already \
escapes (dimension 3 = sp(2,R)). The middle row is the program's flagship \
application under this audit: the analogue-Hawking channel \[LongDash] free \
phase evolution, graybody backscatter as a beamsplitter, pair production at \
the horizon as a two-mode squeezer \[LongDash] is certifiably not \
passive-optics-emulable, even though its correlations, being Gaussian, remain \
efficiently classically simulable. On that channel the two lenses genuinely \
part ways, and both are right \[LongDash] which is precisely why the \
certification battery carries both. Within the class of classical intensity \
emulators, that battery is moreover provably complete \[LongDash] every such \
impostor is either distinguished from a genuine quantum device or certified \
classically bounded \[LongDash] a conditional theorem resting on one stated \
detector-physics premise.\
\>", "Text",ExpressionUUID->"fe6d7216-b951-454b-9d1e-cbd173b9830b"],

Cell[TextData[{
 StyleBox["\[FilledRightTriangle] ",
  FontSize->10,
  FontColor->GrayLevel[0.55]],
 StyleBox["Gaussian states and operations (efficiently classically simulable)",
  FontSize->11,
  FontWeight->"Bold",
  FontColor->GrayLevel[0.3]],
 StyleBox["  \[Bullet] Gaussian, being Gaussian remain efficiently \
classically simulable, Gaussian correlations",
  FontSize->10,
  FontColor->GrayLevel[0.45]]
}], "Text",
 ShowGroupOpener->True,
 CellMargins->{{86, 30}, {1, 
  5}},ExpressionUUID->"7b9f5cae-813f-fd44-baf9-ae42d8d97407"],

Cell["\<\
A Gaussian state of continuous-variable (bosonic) modes is one whose Wigner \
quasiprobability is a Gaussian, so it is fixed entirely by its first moments \
(mean quadratures) and its quadrature covariance matrix; Gaussian operations \
\[LongDash] quadrature-linear maps such as phase shifts, beamsplitters, \
single- and two-mode squeezing, and homodyne measurement \[LongDash] send \
Gaussian states to Gaussian states. Bartlett, Sanders, Braunstein and Nemoto \
(2002) proved that any process built solely from Gaussian preparations, \
operations, and measurements can be simulated on a classical computer in \
polynomial resources. Hence squeezing, though beyond passive optics \
dynamically, keeps the channel's correlations efficiently classically \
simulable.\
\>", "Text",
 CellMargins->{{104, 30}, {4, 4}},
 FontSize->12,ExpressionUUID->"4b890d90-d5ae-0b46-8bd7-e9e30805fbad"],

Cell["\<\
One verdict is deliberately left unclaimed: the whole-mesh one. Block by \
block, every pentagon of a glued mesh passes the genuine-side audit \
\[LongDash] each block carries the same full rotation algebra as the \
single-pentagon cascade \[LongDash] but a single joint verdict for the \
composed device would take either the qubit-side closure (out of reach beyond \
two pentagons: the two-pentagon case already needs roughly 128 GB of memory, \
three pentagons is terabyte-scale \[LongDash] and, worse, non-discriminating \
exactly here, since direct and twisted rings share identical closure growth \
despite opposite gap behavior) or a physically justified optical compilation \
frame for an arbitrary gluing word, which is new research. The polynomial \
passive-versus-active audit above is the instrument built for that job. \
Pointing it at an arbitrary mesh word is the open frontier on the physics \
side of this essay, standing exactly where the ergodic-optimization \
conjecture stands on its mathematics side.\
\>", "Text",ExpressionUUID->"997e016a-54fe-3d4e-b2b7-414c4f9f9884"]
}, Open  ]],

Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell["Concluding Remarks", "Section",ExpressionUUID->"b294f226-72cc-d848-80cb-4b3a38a90d79"],

Cell["\<\
Direct and twisted give exact, opposite-fate closed forms for the two \
simplest gluing choices; searching over gluing words instead of just these \
two finds (ddt)\[Infinity], and two facts true of every word \[LongDash] not \
just the ones tried \[LongDash] show it is provably optimal outside a narrow, \
precisely-bounded window, and a very strong candidate everywhere else. A \
single minimal atom of contextuality, composed by nothing more than a choice \
of how to glue its edges together, turns out to hide a genuinely open problem \
in ergodic optimization.\
\>", "Text",ExpressionUUID->"8275dd4c-cf21-414b-a714-1bfc0b37ab4d"],

Cell["\<\
And the composed object is not an end in itself: as the preceding section \
makes concrete, a glued mesh is a black-box certification instrument \
\[LongDash] its gap density the test's statistical power per pentagon, its \
quantum side a single qutrit at a fixed ~88.5% visibility target, and its \
classical-emulation question audited by an exact polynomial \
passive-versus-active criterion, with the whole-mesh verdict standing as the \
physics-side open frontier.\
\>", "Text",ExpressionUUID->"2d74b655-73b7-ff49-93d2-aa69ae0dafc0"],

Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell["The Ceiling and the Floor", "Subsection",ExpressionUUID->"2c53da89-14ac-c540-a64a-907970230148"],

Cell["\<\
Two facts hold for every gluing word at once, periodic or not, and together \
they close most of the gap the previous section left open. Start with a hard \
ceiling: no matter which word is chosen, \[CurlyTheta]-bar(W) can never \
exceed 3/2. The reason is one accounting trick read two ways: spread weight \
1/2 over every vertex and that alone is already a fractional packing of value \
3/2 per block; spread weight 1/2 instead over each block's two internal edges \
and its two glue-in edges (leaving the shared edge bare) and that's a \
fractional edge cover of the exact same value. Squeezed between a packing and \
a cover of equal size, that value is exact \[LongDash] and since \
\[CurlyTheta] never exceeds a fractional packing number, neither does \
\[CurlyTheta]-bar(W).\
\>", "Text",ExpressionUUID->"455b8140-a5dc-e947-bdb4-d1d0b995fa18"],

Cell["\<\
A second fact is where ddt's real specialness shows up \[LongDash] not in \
beating twisted, but in what it takes to tie it. For every word, \
\[Alpha]-bar(W) \[GreaterEqual] max(4/3, 1 + f_d/2), where f_d is the \
fraction of joints that are direct. Pure twisted (f_d = 0) reaches the 4/3 \
floor exactly, same as ddt; ddt is singled out only by ALSO maximizing the \
direct-fraction at f_d = 2/3 while still sitting on that floor \[LongDash] no \
other word manages both.\
\>", "Text",ExpressionUUID->"e34e36a7-c36c-3044-a8c5-ddcf07397749"],

Cell["\<\
A scoring trick proves it in three connected steps, all checked live in one \
certificate: score each interface state (0, -1/2, -1); every direct or \
twisted transition then gains at least 3/2 or 1 net of the score change, \
giving density \[GreaterEqual] 1 + f_d/2 in general; the same scoring, run \
three direct letters deep, gives a STRICTLY better rate of 13/3 = 3(4/3) + \
1/3 \[LongDash] so hitting the floor exactly rules out three direct letters \
in a row; and at f_d = 2/3 the direct-runs between twisted letters average \
exactly 2, which, with runs of 3 now forbidden, forces every run to equal \
exactly 2 \[LongDash] precisely the definition of (ddt)^k:\
\>", "CodeText",ExpressionUUID->"ecc7a602-49bf-e946-ab6c-88ed16951748"],

Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell["\<\
Module[{phiC = {0, -2^(-1), -1}, phiB = {0, -3^(-1), -2/3}, adjR, Acc, mpMul, \
A3, comb}, adjR[T_, phi_, i_] := Max[Table[T[[i,j]] + phi[[j]] - phi[[i]], \
{j, 3}]]; Acc = Table[dpTransfer[\"d\"][[i,j]] + phiB[[j]] - phiB[[i]], {i, \
3}, {j, 3}]; mpMul[X_, Y_] := Table[Max[Table[X[[i,k]] + Y[[k,j]], {k, 3}]], \
{i, 3}, {j, 3}]; A3 = mpMul[mpMul[Acc, Acc], Acc]; comb = \
AllTrue[Flatten[Table[If[Mod[p, 3] == 0, Module[{ws = Select[Tuples[{\"d\", \
\"t\"}, p], Count[#1, \"d\"] == 2*(p/3) &&  \
!StringContainsQ[StringJoin[StringJoin[#1], StringJoin[#1]], \"ddd\"] & ]}, \
AllTrue[ws, MemberQ[Table[RotateLeft[Characters[StringRepeat[\"ddt\", p/3]], \
r], {r, 0, p - 1}], #1] & ]], True], {p, 3, 12}]], TrueQ]; AllTrue[Range[3], \
adjR[dpTransfer[\"d\"], phiC, #1] >= 3/2 & ] && AllTrue[Range[3], \
adjR[dpTransfer[\"t\"], phiC, #1] >= 1 & ] && Min[Table[Max[A3[[i]]], {i, \
3}]] == 13/3 && comb]\
\>", "Input",
 CellOpen->False,ExpressionUUID->"54dbb0c8-5283-8844-8cd4-7d13abe79e5d"],

Cell[BoxData["True"], "Output",ExpressionUUID->"78d66e44-b2b1-424c-b6b9-11fd4a8909d3"]
}, Open  ]],

Cell["\<\
Put the ceiling and the floor together and the open question shrinks to a \
narrow strip:\
\>", "CodeText",ExpressionUUID->"63372fe3-db70-924c-a1d1-eaf09d505d85"],

Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell["{N[3/2 - (0.0698975), 8], N[0.0714575 - 0.0698975, 6]}", "Input",
 CellOpen->False,ExpressionUUID->"453eb5ee-ee8a-d748-96a6-367a65663959"],

Cell[BoxData[
 RowBox[{"{", 
  RowBox[{"1.4301025`", ",", "0.001559999999999992`"}], "}"}]], "Output",Expre\
ssionUUID->"61d2c341-f24b-2042-8b3c-d39a0a5c3912"]
}, Open  ]],

Cell["\<\
The first number is where the ceiling alone already settles things: for \
\[Alpha]-bar(W) \[GreaterEqual] 1.4301025, \[CurlyTheta]-bar(W) \[LessEqual] \
3/2 forces gap(W) \[LessEqual] 3/2 - \[Alpha]-bar(W) \[LessEqual] gap(ddt), \
so ddt cannot be beaten there \[LongDash] proven, not conjectured. The second \
is the certificate-ladder residual from the previous section, bounding every \
OTHER word to within \[Epsilon] \[TildeTilde] 0.0016 of ddt regardless. \
Between the two, ddt's status is about as settled as a genuinely open problem \
can be \[LongDash] only the low-classical-density, ddt-like words near the \
floor are still undecided.\
\>", "Text",ExpressionUUID->"04cb8a28-7337-e340-af88-c1d71ff97cd1"]
}, Open  ]],

Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell["Future Directions", "Subsection",ExpressionUUID->"e97d921f-ddf3-dc49-a905-a588d15e3ac8"],

Cell["\<\
Four directions follow directly from the boundary of what is proven above:\
\>", "Text",ExpressionUUID->"dbfe4a60-12c0-9849-936a-415afcb2b99b"],

Cell["\<\
Close the residual window: ddt's optimality is proven for \[Alpha]-bar(W) \
\[GreaterEqual] 1.4301025 and certified to within \[Epsilon] \[TildeTilde] \
0.0016 elsewhere; the narrow window \[Alpha]-bar(W) \[Element] [4/3, \
1.4301025) remains open and is the sharpest unresolved piece of the argument.\
\>", "Item",ExpressionUUID->"b07cbea4-d826-8d46-8bd3-97c9b3ace613"],

Cell["\<\
Rule out, or find, an aperiodic competitor: since the finiteness property can \
fail by admitting a non-periodic (Sturmian) maximizer, the natural next step \
is a targeted search or exclusion argument over aperiodic gluing words, not \
further periodic enumeration.\
\>", "Item",ExpressionUUID->"0c1503d4-f1ab-024f-9b1d-428efe95d04f"],

Cell["\<\
Tighten the certificate ladder: extending \[CapitalGamma]_k to larger window \
sizes k shrinks \[Epsilon] further and sharpens the evidence, even though \
\[LongDash] per the obstruction above \[LongDash] no finite window is \
guaranteed to close the gap on its own.\
\>", "Item",ExpressionUUID->"1b9193c6-d3e5-9d4a-980b-5a11a0354803"],

Cell["\<\
Point the physical lens at the composition: the polynomial \
passive-versus-active audit of the closing section decides optical \
emulability for declared designs; extending it to a single joint verdict for \
a whole glued mesh needs a physically justified optical compilation frame for \
arbitrary gluing words \[LongDash] the open frontier where this essay's \
mathematics meets its physics.\
\>", "Item",ExpressionUUID->"fb3347c6-9ae4-c44b-a453-9231b35de398"]
}, Open  ]]
}, Open  ]],

